The term biomass includes a variety of vegetal and animal resources such as crops, agricultural waste, forestry, green plants, but also animal and domestic wastes. Biomass of vegetal origin is present mostly in the form of lignocellulose with an estimated global annual production over 10 billion tons (dry basis).9 The lignocellulosic biomass exploitation is environmentally friendly, which could not only convert the huge amounts of vegetal waste into chemical products, but also diminish the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ emissions. Additionally, employing the non-edible lignocellulosic feedstock, rather than corns or grains for biofuel preparation, could avoid the dilemma of food vs fuel.10 Hence, researchers began to explore facile and affordable technologies for lignocellulosic biomass valorization. As an example, lignocellulosic biomass could be converted into bio-fuels and bio-char through simple pyrolysis at $800{-}1000\ {\mathrm{~}}^{\circ}{\bf C}$ , but the high oxygen contents (up to $40\%$ ) of the raw lignocellulosic biomass and uncontrolled reaction process determine the high acidity and poor quality of the resultant biofuels, making the complex and energy consuming post treatments (e.g. hydrogenation, deoxygenation) indispensable for the subsequent product purification and utilization, leading to the lowered efficiency and enhanced cost in practical applications.11,12 Besides, the lignocellulosic biomass feedstocks could also be transformed into bio-gases (e.g. $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) through the low-cost and low-emission gasification process, but the formation of undesirable tars cannot be entirely avoided.13 Apart from the thermochemical transformation, biological processes have also been investigated for biomass valorization, during which the lignocellulosic biomass is decomposed and converted into biofuels (gas and liquid phase) and chemicals (sugars, alcohols and acids) by the action of enzymes and bacteria. However, the strict reaction conditions, unsatisfactory efficiency and poor recyclability are serious drawbacks for practical large scale applications.14
In summary, the direct transformation of raw lignocellulosic feedstocks is rather difficult, as lignocellulose is composed of lignin $(18\mathrm{-}28\%)$ , cellulose $(40-50\%)$ and hemicellulose $(20-30\%)$ , each class of biopolymer possessing different components, structures and characteristics (Fig. 1).15–17 Lignin is a class of complex cross-linked phenolic polymers constituted by three aromatic monomers including $p$ -coumaryl alcohol, coniferyl alcohol, and sinapyl alcohol. Cellulose is a kind of homopolymer of glucose units. Hemicellulose, in turn, is a mixture of homo- or heteropolysaccharides containing pentoses (xylose, arabinose), hexoses (glucose, mannose, galactose) and their acidic and basic derivatives. Hence, targeting the optimal utilization of each component of lignocellulosic biomass for the large scale preparation of highly useful biochemicals and biofuels, chemical catalysis is considered as the most efficient approach in comparison with thermochemical and biological transformations.18–20 In chemical catalysis, raw lignocellulose feedstocks are firstly subjected to pretreatments (e.g. ball milling, washing and consecutive acidic hydrolysis) to decompose their complex structures, affording platform molecules (e.g. propenyl phenol units, hexose, pentose, furans and other small molecules), which could be subsequently upgraded into a wide range of chemical compounds via various reactions including hydrogenation, oxidation, rearrangement, esterification, hydrodeoxygenation, etc.21 During the upgrading process, chemical
