Similar to the conventional petrochemical industry, both homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts have been developed and applied in biomass valorization. However, strategies to gain selectivity towards the desirable products are in general a remaining challenge in homogeneous catalysis for biomass transformation processes, because the interactions between substrates and catalysts are more difficult to control.24 Besides, reaction workup like separation and purification make homogeneous catalysis less attractive and even more complex and costly for large-scale operation. Additionally, homogeneous catalysts often suffer from the use of toxic metals, strong corrosivity, severe pollution and poor recyclability, making it in some cases more complicate to meet the increasingly strict environmental regulations.24 In this context, heterogeneous catalysts, with active sites and reactants in different phases, can provide an efficient solution to achieve reliable catalyst durability as well as convenient product isolation in biomass transformation. Moreover, the tunable composition, tailorable particle morphology and porous architecture would further endow heterogeneous catalysts with versatile physicochemical parameters that offer high potential for the design of efficient catalytic processes.25 As an example, the nanoscale sizes (1– $100\mathrm{nm}$ ) of active sites endow the catalysts with enriched active sites, unique nanoscale/nanocrystal effects, strengthened interaction specificities, and thus significantly enhanced catalytic reactivity in comparison with their bulk counterparts. In addition, the high surface areas of heterogeneous catalysts are beneficial for the enrichment and exposure of active sites, allowing their accessibility and utilization. Furthermore, the abundant pores as well as channels would also facilitate and control the mass diffusion and sorption, and to some extent modify the product selectivity through steric effects.26 With the rapid development of heterogeneous catalysis, a vast range of different types of catalysts with novel topologies and attractive catalytic performances have been designed and fabricated for biomass transformation, including MOFs, carbon-based materials, zeolites and silicates, metal-oxides, ionic liquids (ILs), layered double hydroxides (LDHs) and polymers.27–36
## 1.2 MOF and MOF-based catalysts
MOFs, also known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), is a class of crystalline materials composed of metal nodes and organic linkers, which have attracted considerable research attention due to their unique properties including high surface areas, modifiable compositions and easy functionalization.37–39 In the past few decades, MOFs and MOF-based materials have been extensively investigated in various frontier domains including catalysis, gas adsorption and separation, hydrogen storage and drug delivery (Fig. 2).40–42 In the year 1994, Ogura et al. for the first time employed the pristine MOF, $\{[\mathrm{Cd}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})_{2}]-$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\big\}_{\infty}$ (bpy: bipyridine), as a heterogeneous catalyst for the liquid-phase cyanosilylation reaction;43 17 years later, MOFs entered the stage of biomass valorization with the first attempt on cellulose hydrolysis over a MOF-based acidic catalyst reported by Kitagawa and co-workers.44 Afterwards, with the rapid development of synthetic strategies and characterization techniques, a large amount of MOF-based catalysts were designed, fabricated and applied in biomass transformation.45–48
In biomass upgrading, the morphology, porosity and composition of the catalysts work synergistically controlling mass diffusion, molecule sorption and catalytic cycles, thereby having a significant impact on the catalytic performance. In this respect, there are sufficient examples in the literature showing that as a consequence of their unique structures and physicochemical properties MOFs and MOF-based materials outperform the performance of conventional porous materials (e.g. zeolites, carbons) in starting material library, ability to control porosity, backbone functionalization, guest immobilization and active site modification. Therefore, MOFs are highly versatile materials, offering several opportunities for the controllable synthesis of highly promising efficient catalysts.49–51 As an example, metal nodes and organic ligands of MOFs could be employed directly or after some post-synthetic treatment introducing groups as acid or base centers (e.g. $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ or redox sites (e.g. Cr, Cu, Ti, Fe, Co sites) able to promote many reactions (e.g. hydrolysis, dehydration, isomerization, condensation). Besides composition, the porosity of MOFs could be fine-tuned by judicious selection of the organic linkers or by tailoring the synthesis parameters to facilitate the diffusion of biomass-derived molecules and to favor the accessibility of active sites which are deposited throughout catalyst matrixes. Moreover, MOFs could be employed as supports or hosts for the uniform immobilization or encapsulation of a large amount of reactive species (e.g. metal and metal oxide nanoparticles (NPs)/clusters, polyoxometalates (POMs), quantum dots, organic and organometallic molecules), preventing them from leaching, agglomeration or deactivation during the reactions. Additionally, MOFs can also serve as sacrificial templates or precursors for the synthesis of metal–carbon composites for particular catalytic applications requiring high thermal stability, high graphitization, specific metallic facets or metal–carbon synergies.52 The above mentioned features endow MOFs and MOF-based materials with suitable and adjustable physicochemical properties for various catalytic biomass reactions, including hydrolysis, dehydration, isomerization, hydrogenation, oxidation, esterification, condensation, rearrangement, polymerization and hydrodeoxygenation. In addition, the highly tunable and predictable features of MOFs can motivate the investigation of structure–activity relationships and molecular simulations, hence providing new pathways for the design and fabrication of novel MOF-based materials with enhanced functionality and improved catalytic efficiency for biomass valorizations.53,54
## 1.3 Scope of this review
This review focuses on the application of pristine MOFs and MOF-based materials (functionalized MOFs, MOF compounds and MOF-derived materials) for biomass valorization. Some early published reviews have already discussed biomass transformations employing MOF-based materials as catalysts. As an example, Janiak et al. summarized MOF-based catalysts for acid catalysis to produce biomass-derived platform chemicals in comparison with zeolites.55 Sels et al. and Astruc et al. reviewed the most significant findings and advances in biomass valorization made by porous materials including functionalized MOFs and MOF-derived catalysts.56,57 Howarth et al. summarized the green applications of MOFs including biomass transformation.58 Wu et al. reviewed MOF-derived catalysts for the valorization of lignocellulose.59 In this review, we aim at presenting a rational approach for the design and preparation of MOF-based catalysts for biomass valorization, the fabrication approaches, followed by a description of structure–performance relationships. Thus, the first section of this review focuses on materials preparation, paying attention to structural MOF adaptation approaches and MOF derived materials that have been employed as catalysts in biomass conversion processes. The second part summarizes reported biomass conversion reactions catalyzed by MOFs. The literature studies have been classified according to the complexity of the catalytic system, starting from those reports using pristine MOF materials and increasing the complexity of the system up to the use of MOF-derived metal and metal oxide nanoparticles wrapped on carbon.
Finally, we present our outlook and perspectives on MOF-based materials in biomass valorizations. Our aim is to stimulate further research on the design and development of MOF-based materials for catalytic biomass transformation and other advanced applications.
## 1.4 Synthetic strategies for MOF-based catalysts
1.4.1 Pristine and functionalized MOFs. In some kinds of MOFs, the metal nodes are weakly coordinated with solvent molecules and could be simply converted into coordination unsaturated metal sites through activation treatments (e.g. heating, degassing).60,61 The obtained metal sites with empty orbitals possess remarkable Lewis acidity and adequate redox reactivity, and hence these materials have been extensively investigated in a large number of biomass transformations including isomerization, dehydration, aldol condensation, and hydrogenation.62 Typically, the MIL-101(Cr), MIL-53(Al), UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , HKUST-1(Cu) and MIL-125(Ti) MOFs, featuring remarkable Lewis acidity, have shown attractive catalytic performance in the hydrolytic degradation of cellulose.
On the other hand, the organic ligands of MOFs offer ample possibilities for the introduction of acid–base functional groups. Noticing that the Brønsted acidic sites are essential for a considerable range of biomass conversions, Kitagawa et al. for the first time designed and fabricated an acid functionalized MOF, MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ , for cellulose hydrolysis (Fig. 3).44 The MIL-101(Cr)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ was directly prepared from the sulfonate substituted terephthalate ligands (monosodium 2-sulfoterephthalic acid), replacing in the MIL-101(Cr) synthesis the pristine terephthalate by its derivative. Afterwards, various substituted organic ligands having various functional groups besides sulfonic acid, like amino, amide, pyridyl, sulfoxy, bipyridyl, etc., have also been utilized for the synthesis of functionalized MOFs, which were subsequently applied in biomass valorization and afforded attractive catalytic performance.63–65 Unfortunately, the uncontrollable assembling processes made it difficult to design or predict the nanoarchitectures and components of the resultant MOFs. Among the various reasons for the lack of predictive design the most important one derives from the fact that the utilization of functionalized ligands significantly affects the solubility of MOF precursors and the subsequent crystallization process. In this regard, post-synthetic strategies, such as ligand modification, ligand exchange and deprotection, still allow rational design and fabrication of MOFs by introducing the functional groups into the pre-synthesized MOF structures.63–68 Moreover, the appropriate combination of the functionalized metal nodes and organic ligands could integrate multiple active sites and facilitate the preparation of multifunctional catalysts especially suited to promote biomass cascade reactions. For instance, Bao et al. reported a bifunctional $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ catalyst for the cascade transformation of glucose into HMF.69 The $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ acted as the Lewis acid and Brønsted acid sites for glucose isomerization and the subsequent fructose dehydration, respectively. The integration of two kinds of active sites not only simplified the operations of the cascade glucose transformation, but boosted the catalytic efficiency, affording up to $45\%$ HMF yield with complete glucose conversion. Additionally, the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ was stable under the investigated reaction conditions, and showed remarkable durability in a fixed bed reactor within 56 hours.
1.4.2 Immobilization and encapsulation of guest molecules. Transition metal NPs have been widely investigated and reported as efficient redox active sites in biomass valorizations.55–57 Downsizing the transition metal particles into nanoscale significantly enhances the metal dispersion and specific surface areas, resulting in an increase in activity of the sites as compared with analogous materials with larger particle size. In this context, the well-ordered nanostructures and porous nature endow MOFs with desirable properties as supports and hosts for the immobilization and encapsulation of a wide range of catalytically active guest molecules, including metal NPs, metal oxides and metal composites.
The impregnation–reduction method is one of the most commonly utilized strategies to incorporate guests inside MOFs.45 During the synthesis, the metal NPs are mostly adsorbed onto the external surfaces of MOF crystals by van der Waals forces and partially incorporated into the MOF channels. The high specific areas, and periodic and porous structures of MOFs largely facilitate the immobilization and uniform distribution of metal NPs, ensuring the high accessibility and homogeneous chemical microenvironment of the active sites, therefore affording a significantly enhanced catalytic reactivity.52 Moreover, the interactions between reactant molecules and part of the MOF crystals also provide alternative possibilities to activate the substrate, facilitating the catalytic reaction. As an example, Li’s group reported a DABCOcontaining multifunctional Pt/Ni-MOF catalyst for hydrogenating coupling of nitriles.70 During the reaction, Ni ions, Pt NPs and dangling DABCO linkers work synergistically in nitrile activation, $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ activation and side reaction suppression, respectively, affording up to ${>}99\%$ yield toward desired secondary imines under optimal reaction conditions. In a different example, the use of multiple metals has additionally induced synergistic effects among different metal species enhancing the catalytic efficiency. Thus, a series of bimetallic $\mathrm{Ru-Pt/MIL}{\cdot}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ catalysts with different $\mathrm{Ru/Pt}$ ratios have been employed for benzene hydrogenation to cyclohexane. The reaction results indicated that the obtained bimetallic catalysts exhibit significantly higher activity in comparison with their monometallic counterparts, which was attributed to the strong change in the metallic properties of Ru–Pt alloys.71
During the preparation of MOF-supported catalysts, the fine metal NPs tend to migrate and aggregate to reduce their surface energy. This undesirable phenomenon would result in the formation of metal clusters or NPs, and hence hinder the uniform dispersion of metal active sites and to some extent diminish the catalytic efficiency.53,54 Moreover, the enlarged particle sizes can also weaken the interactions between active sites and MOF supports, leading to metal leaching and even catalyst deactivation. In this regard, taking advantages of MOFs with tridimensional pore geometry having large cages interconnected through small windows, several encapsulation synthetic strategies were developed to confine the guest species within channels/pores/layers of MOFs, which could precisely confine and isolate these guests, limiting their sizes and impeding their aggregation. Among the synthetic strategies reported for the preparation of MOF-based encapsulated materials, the most general ones are: ship-in-bottle, bottle-around-ship, sandwich assembly and in situ encapsulation approaches (Fig. 4). Accordingly, guests including metal and metal oxide NPs/alloys, quantum dots, POMs, metal organic molecules, organic molecules, metal–organic polyhedral and polymers were precisely encapsulated within MOF channels/ pores, MOF layers and MOF bulks with rationally designed spatial dispersions.72,73
Ship-in-bottle. The ship-in-bottle strategy is a post-synthetic methodology encompassing many synthetic approaches, like impregnation, co-precipitation, deposition–precipitation, chemical vapor deposition, solution infiltration, surface grafting, solid grinding and microwave irradiation, aiming to introduce guest molecules into the pores of the pre-assembled MOFs.74–76 Very recently, Li et al. demonstrated a facile and general strategy to immobilize a series of ultrafine bimetal alloys within the MOF cavities with high loadings and uniform dispersions (Fig. 5).83 As exemplified by the synthesis of $\mathrm{Cu-Pd}\ @\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ nanostructures, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ was initially impregnated into the cavities of $\mathbf{MIL-101}$ and subsequently reduced into ultrafine Cu NPs by $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Then, with the addition of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ precursors under high intensity ultrasonic irradiation, the encapsulated Cu NPs were partially corroded via galvanic replacement to obtain ultrafine Cu–Pd alloys. Noteworthily, following a similar methodology, nine kinds of bimetallic alloys in total could be successfully obtained immobilized within MIL-101 cavities using $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}$ as transition metal and noble metal precursors, respectively. The obtained bimetallic alloys were highly dispersed and well alloyed with ultrafine sizes $\left(1.1-2.2\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ and high loadings (up to $10.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ).
However, the control of the spatial dispersion of guest molecules during the synthesis still remains a challenge, as they tend to aggregate or deposit on the external surface of the MOF matrix. In this regard, inspired by the different hydrophilic nature of the external and internal surfaces in some MOF crystals (e.g. MIL-101(Cr), UiO-67(Zr)), a double-solvent method (DSM) was proposed by Xu and co-workers to exclusively immobilize the guest molecules on the internal surfaces of the MOF matrix (Fig. 6).77 Typically, a quantitative volume of aqueous solution containing the metal precursor such as $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ was injected into a large volume of hexane suspension containing the MIL-101(Cr) host $(V_{\mathrm{aqueous}}\leq V_{\mathrm{MOFpores}})$ . Then, the aqueous solution was absorbed into MIL-101(Cr) pores driven by the capillary force, resulting in the exclusive deposition of Pt ions into the hydrophilic internal surfaces. Finally, the Pt precursors were in situ converted into small Pt NPs $\left(\sim1.8\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ under ${\bf H}_{2}/{\bf H}\mathrm{e}$ flow without obvious aggregation even at high loadings (up to $5\ \mathrm{wt\%}\$ ). On this basis, several kinds of noble and non-noble metal based metal $\mathbf{NPs}\ @\mathbf{MOF}$ composites were prepared.50 Noteworthily, in order to avoid metal aggregation under the harsh reduction conditions and further diminish the metal NP sizes, Li et al. demonstrated a photoreduction method to reduce the Pd precursors under much greener and milder conditions.78 The obtained Pd NPs featured significantly small sizes (ca. $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) at a low metal loading $\left(0.67~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ .
Apart from the introduction of mono- and multiple kinds of metal species into MOF cavities, Xu’s group focused on the size modification of the encapsulated metal alloys and combined this DSM approach with a concentration-controlled reduction treatment to synthesize a series of encapsulated AuNi alloy NPs (Fig. 7).79 When the concentration of the reductant $\left(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\right)$ increased from $_{0.2\textbf{M}}$ to $_{0.4\mathrm{~M~}}$ and $0.6\textbf{M}$ , AuNi alloys were obtained in sizes of $5.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $2.0{-}5.0~\mathrm{nm}$ and $1.8~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. On the other hand, Li et al. demonstrated a seed-mediated growth approach for the preparation of $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ core–shell NPs within the MOFs using molecular hydrogen as the reductant (Fig. 8).80 First, Pd NPs were introduced into the cavities of a pre-assembled MOF host as seeds. Then, molecular hydrogen was adsorbed on the surface of these seeds and dissociated into activated hydrogen atoms for the subsequent deposition and reduction of Ag species. The key step leading to the success of this seed-mediated growth was attributed to the dissociation of molecular hydrogen over the Pd NPs, which largely facilitates the selective deposition of Ag, meanwhile preventing the undesirable self-nucleated generation of Ag NPs. Accordingly, the obtained $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ core shell NPs were of size $2.6{-}3.1~\mathrm{nm}$ with tunable $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{Ag}$ molar ratios (e.g. 30, 20, 10 and 0). Later, this DSM approach was further developed by Jiang’s group to modify the morphologies of the encapsulated metal NPs through the fabrication of $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{Co}$ core–shell NPs in MIL-101(Cr) pores.81 The key process for the successful formation of $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{Co}$ core–shell nanostructure was the pre-incorporation of Pd and Co precursors within MIL-101(Cr) pores and the in situ selective reduction by ${\bf N H}_{3}{\bf B H}_{3}$ according to the order of the metal standard redox potential. Recently, Li’s group extended the guest molecule scope of this hydrophilicity-directed approach and reported the successful encapsulation of metal–organic polyhedra (MOP) within MOF pores.82 The encapsulation process was exemplified using $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ (M and L stand for ( $\mathrm{\bf{\dot{n}}})\mathrm{\bf{Pd}}(\mathrm{\bf{NO}}_{3})_{2}$ and 1,3,5-tris(4-pyridyl)-2,4,6- triazine, respectively) and MIL-101(Cr) as the model MOP guest and MOF host, respectively. In this two-step synthesis, M aqueous solution was directly injected into a hexane solution containing MIL-101(Cr) and L $(V_{\mathrm{Msolution}}<V_{\mathrm{poreofMOF}})$ under stirring. Driven by the capillary force, the aqueous solution was precisely adsorbed into the MOF pores with hydrophilic inner surfaces and assembled with L to form $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ polyhedra. This was the first example of encapsulating into a MOF a guest molecule whose molecular dimension is larger than the aperture size.
In contrast to the above mentioned protocols which involved the introduction of metal precursors into the pre-formed MOF hosts, Li and co-workers reported a unique approach for guest molecule encapsulation through anchoring the precursors onto organic linkers followed by the MOF synthesis.84 This novel approach would efficiently avoid the effect of different diffusion resistance of MOF internal and external surfaces and facilitate the guest encapsulation within MOF cavities. As exemplified by the synthesis of $\mathbf{Pd\@UiO-67}$ , the $\mathrm{Pd}^{4+}$ ions were anchored onto the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid to form the [ $(5,5^{\prime}$ -dicarboxy$^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine)palladium $\left[\mathrm{II}\right]$ dichloride $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\right)$ composite, which was then directly utilized together with $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{bpdc}}$ as mixed ligands for the assembly of $\mathbf{Pd\@UiO-67}$ via a solvothermal process and reduction treatment. Notably, the as-synthesized Pd@UiO-67 possessed uniform and homogeneous distribution of Pd NPs of $3.0\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ size and tunable Pd loadings, which could be easily modified through variation of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}/\mathbf{H}_{2}$ bpdc ratio. Another efficient protocol to introduce metal NPs into MOF cavities is to anchor metal precursors onto the functional groups of MOF backbones. Lin et al. successively coordinated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ species onto bpy groups and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(\upmu_{3}-O)_{4}(\upmu_{3}-O H)_{4}}$ subunits of the UiO-bpy host, respectively.85 After reduction under hydrogen flow, ultrasmall $\mathrm{{Cu/ZnOx}}$ NPs $(<1~\mathrm{{nm})}$ were in situ generated in UiO-bpy cages. Following a similar protocol, Pt NPs in $2{\cdot}3~\mathrm{nm}$ and $5{\mathrm{-}}6~\mathrm{nm}$ sizes were successfully introduced and in situ generated within the cages of functionalized MOF-1 and MOF-2 hosts, respectively, via a MOF-mediated photoreduction of $\mathrm{{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}}$ .
Bottle-around-ship. The bottle-around-ship strategy is a de novo strategy to fabricate the MOF host around the guest molecules, and it often consists of two steps: preparation of the well-defined guest molecules and overgrowth of the MOF layers.53,86,87 Noteworthily, in this strategy, the guest size is no longer restricted to the MOF pore dimensions, but bulky guests could be covered with a MOF matrix to form core@shell structures. The preparation process of guests offers ample opportunities to modify their morphology, spatial dispersion and composition, the key feature for success being avoidance of guest aggregation. There are still some remaining challenges that have to be overcome, such as the tendency of metal NPs to undergo aggregation due to their high surface energies and the effect of the addition of MOF precursors, mostly soluble metal salts, promoting aggregation due to shielding of the charge repulsion. Therefore, it is essential to add an extra charge or steric repulsion to the reactants in order to get well-defined and uniformly distributed guest molecules. Hence, capping agents or surfactants are generally employed in most cases to stabilize the guests and to facilitate the overgrowth of the MOF matrix.
As an example, Tang et al. synthesized a $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Ir}\mathbf{MOF}{-}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ core–shell nanoarchitecture through a mixed solvothermal method using polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as the surfactant in the preparation processes of both Pd NPs and the IrMOF-3(Zn) matrix.88 Benefiting from the stabilization and affinity effect of PVP, the obtained materials possessed uniform and monodisperse spherical morphology with Pd NPs as a core of about $\sim35\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter and IrMOF-3(Zn) shells of $\sim145\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness. Moreover, the double solvent system consisting of $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and ethanol also effectively hindered the self-aggregation and favored the homogeneous growth of IrMOF- $3(\mathrm{Zn})$ on the Pd NPs. Similarly, through a twostep synthesis protocol, uniform Ag nanowires with diameters of $90{-}120\ \mathrm{nm}$ were coated with PVP and subsequently encapsulated within ZIF-8(Zn) shells of $50\mathrm{-}63\quad\mathrm{nm}$ thickness. ZIF-8(Zn) shells only grow selectively on the PVP-coated Ag nanowires. Based on this precedent, Duan and co-workers demonstrated a versatile polydopamine (PDA) assisted approach for the encapsulation of various inorganic/organic NPs (e.g. Au NPs, Au nanostars, mesoporous silica NPs and polystyrene NPs) within diverse MOFs (e.g. ZIF-8(Zn) and UiO-66(Zr)).89
Differently from the above mentioned protocols, in this approach, the PDA surfactant was used to bind the internal NP cores and external MOF shells to form NP@PDA@MOF sandwich nanostructures (Fig. 9). Owing to the good capability of PDA in forming conformal coatings on inorganic/organic NPs and directing the heterogeneous nucleation and overlaying of MOFs, a series of NP@PDA@MOF nanostructures with tunable thickness of PDA and MOF layers were successfully prepared. In addition to the polymer surfactants, organic ligand molecules (e.g. CTAB, MBA and NPT) were also suitable and applicable to suppress metal aggregation and promote the overgrowth of MOF shells. Tsung’s group employed selfassembled CTAB as bridging ligands for the formation of well-defined Pd NPs and the overgrowth of MOF shells.90 The resultant composites featured single Pd nanocrystals embedded individually within single crystalline MOFs, observing the alignment of the $\left\{100\right\}$ planes of Pd NPs and $\{110\}$ planes of MOFs.
Sandwich assembly. Mass diffusion plays a key role in liquid phase reactions, affecting the interactions between the reactants and the active sites, and thereby has an important influence on catalytic activity and selectivity. In this regard, sandwiching guests between the MOF layers could facilitate mass diffusion, while modulating the microenvironment around the active sites. Therefore, guest incorporation between MOF layers can serve to achieve the goal of boosting the reaction rate and tailoring the selectivity towards target products. The preparation of the sandwich-like structure involves guest deposition onto pre-synthesized MOF cores and a subsequent overgrowth process of the MOF shells. This facile process provides new opportunities for the preparation of various nanostructures with tunable properties in size, composition and morphology.72
After the pioneering work by Zeng et al. who prepared ultrafine Au and Ag NPs on designated crystallographic planes of a ZIF-8 matrix to form ZIF-8@metal@ZIF-8 hybrids,91 enormous efforts have been devoted to exploring efficient synthetic approaches to encapsulate guests like metal composites (NPs, oxides, layers), biomacromolecules (proteins, DNAs), LDHs and ILs within MOF layers, resulting in the preparation of a wide range of sandwich nanostructures.92–99 As an example, Tang’s group reported a series of sandwich-like MIL-101@Pt@MIL-101 containing Fe or Cr ions as metal nodes through a simple solvothermal method (Fig. 10).100 Typically, the octahedral MIL-101 cores were pre-synthesized with sizes of 296 and $243\ \mathrm{nm}$ for MIL-101(Fe) and MIL-101(Cr), respectively. After the immobilization of Pt NPs (PVP stabilized, ca. $2.8~\mathrm{nm}$ ), an additional MIL-101(Cr or Fe) shell was coated onto the surface of cores under the same solvothermal conditions. The obtained sandwich-like MIL-101@Pt@MIL-101 nanostructures possessed uniform distributed Pt NPs with tunable loadings and variable MIL-101 shell thickness. Additionally, this approach was also effective for the fabrication of different sandwich-like $\mathbf{MOF^{1}@}$ $\mathbf{MOF}^{2}@\mathbf{MOF}^{1}$ nanostructures composed of different MOF cores and shells (e.g. UiO-66(Zr), UiO-67 $(\mathrm{zr})$ , MOF-74(Co), and MOF$525\mathrm{(Zr)})$ . In another work, employing a micro- and mesoporous polymer composed of porphyrin conjugated $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ species (FeP-CMP) as external shells, Tang et al. successfully demonstrated the preparation of a sandwich-like asymmetric MIL-101@Pt@FePCMP nanohybrid with modifiable shell thickness, further extending the substrate scope, and verified the versatility of this approach even for the preparation of three-component sandwich-like constructs, one of the components being a MOF.101
Hard template. The hard template method is a wellestablished approach for the encapsulation of various guests within hollow MOF shells to prepare core $\textcircled{2}$ shell or yolk@shell nanostructures. Similar to the surfactant assistant strategy, hard templates were employed as the structuring agent for the stabilization and isolation of guests as well as the subsequent overgrowth of MOF shells. Noteworthily, as compared with the surfactant agents, the hard templates are often much easier to be completely removed via solvent dissolution or acid/base etching.54,87 Generally, the removal of hard templates could result in the generation of hollow cavities or hierarchical pores inside the MOF particles, which would effectively enhance the accessibility of the encapsulated active sites and mass diffusion during the liquid phase reaction. The key issue of this method is the selection of easily available and effective hard templates to simplify the synthesis protocol and promote the encapsulation and exposure of guests as active sites.
$\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ is a versatile hard template for the encapsulation of various guests to prepare yolk–shell nanostructures.102 As an example, Su et al. employed $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as the sacrificial-template for the preparation of Au@ZIF-8(Zn) nanoreactors.103 In detail, Au NPs were initially coated with silica to prepare Au@silica hybrids, over which ZIF-8 shells subsequently grew. Finally, after the etching of silica layers, yolk–shell Au@ZIF-8(Zn) nanoreactors were obtained. The hollow cavities and steric hindrance effect of the ZIF-8 shell endowed the as-prepared nanoreactors with remarkably enhanced accessibility to the Au NPs and promoted mass transfer kinetics of both reactants and products. Furthermore, an in situ self-template coordination replication strategy was proposed by Li et al. for the encapsulation of Pt NPs within the 3D-ordered macroporous (3DOM) host (Fig. 11).104 In detail, a pre-synthesized colloidal crystal template composed of polystyrene spheres (PS) was immersed into a methanol–ethylene glycol solution containing $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , tetraethoxysilane-derived $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{PtCl}_{6}$ for the synthesis of an intermediate hybrid, namely, precursor $\textcircled{\mathbf{\hat{a}}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{S}}$ . Then, the precursor $\textcircled{\mathbf{\hat{a}}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{S}}$ was calcined to remove the PS spheres, while converting Zn, Si and Pt precursors into $\mathrm{ZnO/SiO}_{2}$ oxides and metallic Pt NPs, respectively. Finally, the resultant $3\mathrm{DOM-Pt}/$ $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}/\mathbf{S}\mathbf{i}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was immersed into a ${\bf D M F-H}_{2}{\bf O}$ solution containing 2-methylimidazole for the crystallization of ZIF-8 over the $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ scaffolds, thereby resulting in the in situ encapsulation of Pt NPs. TEM and SEM images verified that the ZIF-8 crystals were in situ fabricated on the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}/\mathbf{SiO}_{2}$ scaffolds encapsulating the Pt NPs and forming the $\mathrm{3DOM–Pt@ZIF–8/SiO_{2}}$ nanostructures. Noteworthily, the resultant $\mathrm{3DOM–Pt@ZIF–8/SiO_{2}}$ exhibited a significantly enhanced selectivity in the size-selective hydrogenation reaction, affording ca. 4 fold enhanced conversion of $n$ -hexene in comparison with cis-cyclooctene, which was attributed to the molecule sieving effect of the porous ZIF-8.
Metal oxides could also play the role of hard templates. Generally, the guests are successively coated with metal oxides and MOF layers to form guest@metal oxide@MOF hybrids, and the yolk–shell nanostructures are obtained after the removal of the metal oxide templates. Versatile synthesis strategies were reported by Tsung et al. and Huo et al. employing $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the hard template for the confinement of metal composites (including nanocrystals, NPs, nanorods and nanocubes) within ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn})$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ (BTC: 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid), respectively.105,106 Besides, Yaghi et al. also employed $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ as the template for the preparation of mesoscopic constructs $\mathbf{O}_{\mathrm{h}}$ -nano-Ag@MOF(Al) $\mathrm{{\dot{\langle}O_{h}}}$ meaning octahedral) with a tunable thickness of MOF shells from 10 to $50~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .107
On the other hand, with the development of the hard template method, researchers found that the MOF crystals themselves could also play the role of hard templates. Based on various thermal/chemical stabilities of different kinds of MOFs, MOF-based yolk–shell nanostructures could be directly obtained after the selective removal of the inner ‘‘less stable’’ MOFs in pre-synthesized inhomogeneous MOF hybrids. In this regard, Huo et al. demonstrated for the first time the synthesis of single-crystalline multi-shelled hollow MOF nanostructures through a two-step self-templated strategy (Fig. 12).108 As exemplified by the synthesis of single-shelled MIL-101(Cr) nanostructures, an inhomogeneous MOF precursor composed of two MIL-101(Cr) layers (a ‘‘less stable’’ internal layer and another ‘‘more stable’’ single crystal outer layer) was presynthesized via the step-by-step growth under different conditions to modify the chemical stability of each MIL-101 layer. The resultant precursor was then treated with acetic acid for the selective removal of the relatively ‘‘less stable’’ MIL-101 layers. After centrifugation and washing, the residual single-shelled hollow MIL-101(Cr) crystals were obtained and denoted as SSHM. The double- and triple-shelled MIL-101(Cr) (DSHM and TSHM, respectively) were prepared through repeating cycles of growth and subsequent etching. The cavity sizes (70, 110, and $140~\mathrm{nm}$ ) and shell thickness (25, 45, and $80~\mathrm{nm}$ ) of the as-prepared nanostructures could be easily modified by tuning the reaction parameters (i.e. etching time and reactant concentration). Moreover, the unique multi-shelled nanostructure of the triple-shelled MIL-101(Cr) efficiently contributed to increase its catalytic activity in styrene oxidation, affording ca. 5 fold enhancement in comparison with that of the bulk solid counterparts.
In situ encapsulation. With the concern of scale-up production, aimed at simplifying complex procedures and minimization of the time/cost of the preparation processes, researchers have attempted to develop one-pot synthesis protocols, which could balance the formation of guests and crystallization of MOF shells at the same time. In one-pot synthesis, the key to success is to modulate the reaction parameters (e.g. temperature, solvents, precursors, and surfactant agents) to precisely control the self-nucleation of the guest, overgrowth of the MOF matrix and the integration of them into a single nanostructure.
Tang et al. demonstrated the preparation of core@shell $\mathbf{Au}\ @\mathbf{MOF}\cdot5(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanostructures through a one-pot synthesis (Fig. 13).109 In detail, the Au and MOF precursors involving $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ , PVP, $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{BDC}$ $\mathrm{\bf~\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ : benzenedicarboxylic acid) ligands were mixed and added to the DMF– ethanol solvent for a solvothermal process, during which the MOF-5 shells in situ assembled and encapsulated the PVPstabilized Au NPs to form the $\mathbf{Au}(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{MOF}{\cdot}5(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanostructures with a single Au core coated with a uniform MOF-5 shell. Through tuning the solvothermal parameters, Au NP sizes $\left(30\mathrm{-}54\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ and corresponding MOF-5 shell thickness (3.2 to $69\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) could be easily tailored. Soon later, Li and co-workers reported a slightly modified protocol for the one-step encapsulation of multiple Pd NPs within ${\bf U i O-}67({\bf Z r})$ .110 The Pd precursor, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ ion, $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}\right)$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ were suspended in DMF to form a clear solution. Benefiting from the strong coordination of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ bpydc ligands, the $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ions were stabilized and directly introduced into the crystallization of the UiO-67(Zr) matrix. The obtained Pd NPs of $3.2~\mathrm{nm}$ were uniformly distributed throughout the MOF matrix at $1\mathrm{\mol\%}$ loading. In another work, Li et al. reported a temperature induced one-step approach to fabricate Pd@UiO-67 nanostructures.111 After the mixing of Pd and MOF precursors at $80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the incorporated Pd ions were subsequently reduced by DMF at $130\ {^{\circ}\mathrm{C}},$ , yielding the (111) facet exposed Pd NPs with sizes of $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ incorporated within UiO-67(Zr) shells.
The one-pot hydrothermal approach is also effective at confining and stabilizing metal hybrids like POMs and metal phosphates for the enhancement of their stabilities. Su and co-workers for the first time reported the encapsulation of different Keggin POMs into the Cu-BTC pores for the synthesis of a series of $\mathbf{POM@MOF}$ composites $\mathrm{([Cu_{2}(B T C)_{4/3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{6}}.$ $\left[\mathrm{H}_{n}\mathrm{XM}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\right]\cdot\left(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{N}\right)_{2}$ $\mathbf{\left(X\right)}=\mathbf{\left.Si\right|}$ , Ge, P, As; $\textbf{M}=\textbf{W}$ , Mo)) as heterogeneous catalysts.112 The resultant Keggin polyanions were well confined and alternately arrayed within the Cu-BTC cages without significant conglomerations even at high loadings (up to $47\%$ ). As a result of POM site isolation and porosity, the resulting POM@MOF exhibited remarkably promoted catalytic performance as a heterogeneous catalyst. Noteworthily, in the reusability test, the catalyst was recovered from the reaction suspensions through filtration and reused after washing and heating treatments; neither POM leaching nor activity loss could be observed even after 15 recycles.
Ligand coordination. Apart from the supporting and encapsulation strategies, ligand coordination is another surfactantfree efficient method for the integration of guests within MOF hosts.113,114 Coordinating guests (including metal NPs and organic functional groups) onto MOF ligands can minimize their mobility and enhance the catalytic reactivity and stability. Otherwise, the synergistic interactions between guests and organic ligands can also tailor the local geometry and the electronic densities, and hence specifically modify the selectivity towards target products. Jiang and co-workers employed 1,10- phenanthroline-3,8-dicarboxylic acid (PDC) as a bifunctional ligand for the assembly of a PDC-containing Zr-MOF.115 The PDC ligands not only enhanced the stability of the Zr-MOF, but also provided phenanthroline units for the subsequent incorporation of $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ species. The as-prepared Pd catalysts showed remarkably enhanced turnover number (TON) values and largely reduced consumption of electron-transfer mediators in the oxidative desulfitative coupling in comparison with their homogeneous counterparts. Alternatively, Corma et al. fabricated a thioalkyl chain decorated MOF, $\{\mathbf{Ca}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{Cu}_{6}^{\mathrm{II}}[(S,S)\cdot$ methoxy $\mathrm{\mathrm{7J}_{3}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)\mathrm{\}}.16\mathrm{H}_{2}O\mathrm{_{2}}$ as the host for the gram scale production of sub-nanometer $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ units.116 The $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pt}}^{2+}$ precursors were firstly immobilized onto the S atoms of the MOF skeleton and then reduced by $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}}}$ to form sub-nanometer $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ units, which were homogeneously encapsulated within the MOF channels at a high loading (up to $17\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The resultant $\mathbf{Pt}_{2}^{0}$ -MOF materials exhibited remarkable catalytic activity in $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}C N}$ production under relatively mild reaction conditions (i.e. room temperature).
1.4.3 Pyrolysis. Owing to their highly ordered and periodic nanostructures, MOFs can be employed as sacrificial templates for the preparation of carbon-based composites via pyrolysis.117 During the pyrolysis, the MOF ligands decompose and the atomically dispersed metal nodes are transformed into metal (or metal oxides) embedded in the carbon matrix derived from the organic ligands of the MOF. The physicochemical properties of the MOF-derived components like topological morphology, porosity, component, metal NP size and facet can be precisely tailored through functionalizing the MOF precursor or changing the pyrolysis conditions.118,119 Hence, diverse approaches for the preparation of MOF-derived materials have already been widely reported using different MOFs as templates under various pyrolysis conditions. These MOFderived materials, possessing unique topological morphologies, hierarchical porosity, heteroatom doping, tunable metal NP sizes, strong synergistic interactions and high thermal and chemical stabilities, have exhibited attractive performances in various edge fields including biomass-related catalysis.57,120,121
Direct pyrolysis. After the pioneering work by Xu et al. who reported for the first time the preparation of porous carbons containing metal NPs employing MOF-5 as the sacrificial template,122 a large amount of carbon-based composites have been prepared through the direct pyrolysis of Co-, Cu-, Fe-, Ni-, Mn- and Mo-based MOF templates and the resulting metal NP–carbon composites have been employed for biomass transformation including hydrogenation, oxidation, esterification, etc.38,118–121 Owing to their high specific areas, tunable metal loadings, strong interactions between the metal and carbon matrix, homogeneous heteroatom doping, enhanced chemical and thermal stability, the resultant MOF-derived composites exhibited remarkably high catalytic performance in biomassrelated conversions in comparison with the corresponding conventionally prepared metal NPs supported on carbons.
Li et al. prepared a series of Co-based catalysts $\left(\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{C-N-}\boldsymbol{x}\right)$ through the direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 templates at 600 to $900^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ under argon flow.122 Benefiting from the reduction and separation effect of the ligand derived carbons, the resultant Co NPs were well isolated by the graphitized carbon without significant aggregation. Accordingly, the Co NPs of the as-prepared $\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ $x=600$ , 700 and 800) ranged from 9 to $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ in size, and were much smaller than other Co-based counterparts prepared through simple physical mixing $\left(20-30~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ . Consequently, the obtained Co catalysts exhibited significantly enhanced activity in the oxidative esterification of a wide scope of aromatic alcohols (e.g. furfural, HMF), affording up to $99\%$ yields toward the corresponding target esters under relatively mild conditions (room temperature, $0.1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ air without base additions), which outperformed most of the non-noble metal catalysts which had been reported in the literature. Alternatively, using the monometallic Co-, Ni- and bimetallic CoNi-, CoCu-, NiCu-based MOFs as templates, a variety of metal@carbon composites were prepared through similar direct pyrolysis, which exhibited good to excellent reactivity in heterogeneous catalysis including nitrile transfer hydrogenation, alkane oxidation, alcohol oxidation, aldehyde oxidative amidation and transfer hydrogenation reactions.123–127 Noteworthily, the direct pyrolysis method is also efficient for the preparation of single atom catalysts. Li’s group developed a Co-based single atom catalyst through the direct pyrolysis of the bimetallic $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn/Co}}$ ZIF with homogeneously distributed Zn and Co nodes. The pyrolysis was carried out at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , during which the Zn species were gradually evaporated away, leaving the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ single atoms homogeneously embedded in the 2-methylimidazole ligand derived carbon matrix.128
In addition to direct pyrolysis, several de novo and post treatments like linker coordination, galvanic replacement, and acid washing have been explored to introduce extra metal species as active sites into MOF-derived carbon-based composites for the preparation of multiple metal based catalysts.37 Taking advantage of the differences in the standard reduction potentials of various metal/metal ion pairs, the galvanic replacement method was developed as an effective approach to introduce and deposit noble metal NPs onto MOF-derived metal NPs. Up to now, a large amount of MOF-derived multiple metal based nanocomposites including core@shell, metal alloy, and metal clusters have been reported.129–131 On the other hand, the facile linker coordination and acid washing treatments were also effective at controlling the distribution and decreasing the metal NP size of MOF-derived catalysts. As an example, Beller et al. demonstrated the preparation of Co-based single atom catalysts through the dispersion of pre-synthesized Co-MOFs (Co-DABCO-TPA) onto commercial Vulcan XC 72R carbon powder, followed by pyrolysis under an argon atmosphere (Fig. 14).132 Jiang et al. proposed a novel mixed-ligand method for the synthesis of MOF-derived Fe single atom catalysts.133 Additionally, Li and co-workers reported a coordination-assisted strategy involving the anchorage of $\mathrm{{Ru}}^{3+}$ precursors onto the free $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ groups at the $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ linker and the subsequent pyrolysis and acid etching treatments for the preparation of single atomic Ru sites embedded in N-doped carbons.134 Despite the breakthroughs in the preparation of single atom or atomically dispersed metal catalysts, to the best of our knowledge, the field of application of MOF-derived single atom catalysts in biomass valorizations is still under development.
Pyrolysis with reducing agents. Tailoring the pyrolysis atmosphere has been demonstrated as an effective approach to modify the morphology of the metal species.135–137 Using MIL-88B as the sacrificial template, Li and coworkers demonstrated a carbon monoxide oriented pyrolysis approach for the preparation of monodisperse octahedral ferrite nanocrystals (Fig. 15).138 In brief, MIL-88B was pre-synthesized through a solvothermal method and then calcined at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a CO–Ar atmosphere containing certain CO volume ratios. TEM characterization suggested that the introduction of CO would effectively regulate the size and morphology of the resultant $\mathrm{FeO}_{x}/\mathrm{C}$ materials. Interestingly, when the CO volume ratio reached $50\%$ , the $\mathrm{FeO}_{x}/\mathrm{C}$ ( $\mathrm{\tilde{\Delta}V_{C O}/V_{A r}=1}]$ material was obtained with monodisperse ferrite octahedral nanocrystals with a typical edge length of ca. $90\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Based on the characterization data, the authors proposed that the morphology regulation effect was ascribed to the preferential adsorption of CO molecules on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}(111)$ facets, which effectively hindered their further growth and hence preserved them in the resultant nanocrystals, which was further confirmed by DFT calculations. The catalytic performance of the obtained $\mathrm{FeO}_{x}/\mathrm{C}$ 1 $\left(\mathrm{{V_{CO}}/\mathrm{{V_{Ar}}=1}}\right)$ was subsequently tested in the aerobic oxidation of 5-hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF) into 2,5-diformylfuran (DFF), reaching nearly quantitative DFF yield. ATR-IR spectra and DFT calculations indicated that the high selectivity towards DFF was mainly attributed to the relatively strong interactions of $-\mathbf{OH}$ in comparison with $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ groups over the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}(111)$ facets of the catalyst, which favored rapid DFF desorption and thereby hindered undesirable side reactions.
Apart from the one-step approach, Li et al. also proposed an atmosphere oriented two-step pyrolysis-oxidation protocol for the preparation of MOF-derived Co-based NPs with tunable morphologies (including core–shell $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , yolk@shell $\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{\overline{{a}}})\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , and hollow $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ NPs) embedded in N-doped hollow carbon polyhedra.139 In the synthesis, Co-based ZIF-67s with polyhedral morphologies of size ca. $900\ \mathrm{nm}$ were selected as the sacrificial templates. The direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 templates was carried out at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{Ar}$ flow for the synthesis of solid Co NPs embedded in hollow N-doped carbon matrixes (Co/HNCP) as the intermediate nanostructure. Subsequently, $\mathbf{Co/HNCP}$ oxidation was carried out under an oxidative atmosphere to synthesize a series of Co-based NPs with different morphologies, which could be precisely regulated by controlling the oxidation time. As a consequence, core–shell $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4};$ yolk–shell $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ , and hollow $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ NPs were obtained after an oxidation process within 20, 40 and 60 minutes, respectively. In addition, Li et al. additionally reported a reduction–oxidation strategy for the synthesis of hollow $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ NPs embedded in hollow carbons.140 In brief, ZIF-67 was prepared as a precursor and calcined successively under $\mathbf{Ar/H}_{2}$ $\left(10\%\mathrm{~H}_{2}\right)$ 1 and air atmospheres, during which the Co species were initially reduced and then oxidized to form hollow $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ NPs embedded in the ligand derived hollow carbon matrix.
Confined pyrolysis. Nanoconfinement is also considered as an effective protocol to prepare ultrafine metal species minimizing the metal migration and aggregation that can appear in the direct pyrolysis of MOF templates.37 In general, nanoscale MOFs or MOF precursors were introduced into cavities/channels of porous supports (e.g. mesoporous silica, zeolite) to fabricate a hybrid composite. Subsequently, the as-prepared composite was employed as the sacrificial template, undergoing a calcination process to in situ convert the MOF precursors into ultrafine metal species confined in porous hosts. The periodic channels of porous supports can efficiently restrict the metal aggregation, enhance the accessibility and the stability of the ultrafine metal species, and hence promote their catalytic performance in liquid phase reactions. De Vos et al. reported a solid-state crystallization approach to introduce the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ -based MOF UiO- $66\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ within the channels of mesoporous SBA-15 for the synthesis of a UiO- $66\mathrm{(NH_{2})@S B A-15}$ hybrid catalyst.141 The obtained catalyst exhibited remarkably enhanced accessibility and durability of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ active sites in the liquid phase transformation of large molecules with low diffusion coefficients (e.g. steroids). Han et al. prepared sub-nanometer Ni and Co NP catalysts supported on $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ skeletons using MOFs and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as a precursor and hard template, respectively (Fig. 16).142 As exemplified by the preparation of $\mathbf{Ni}/\mathbf{SiO}_{2}$ , initially, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathbf{\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathbf{\mathrm{{BTC}}}}$ and TEOS were added to a mixed solvent consisting of 1-octyl-3- methylimidazolium perchlorate, triethylammonium nitrate and water for the fabrication of a $\mathbf{Ni-BTC/SiO}_{2}$ hybrid. After solvent removal and a reduction process, $\mathbf{Ni}/\mathbf{SiO}_{2}$ was obtained with ultrafine Ni NPs $\left(c a.\ 0.9\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ homogeneously distributed throughout the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ support even at a high loading (up to $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ).
Self-templated pyrolysis. Hollow nanostructures have received considerable attention as their morphology and unique voids could largely facilitate mass diffusion and enhance the stability of the active sites of the catalyst. The most commonly used template-assisted approaches are effective, but still suffer from complex template introduction and removal, non-uniform coatings and limited conditions of template compatibility. In this regard, MOF hybrids, possessing predictable morphology and tunable composition, offer a wide range of versatile sacrificial templates for the fabrication of hollow yolk–shell structures. In recent years, a MOF oriented self-templated pyrolysis approach was proposed for the preparation of various yolk–shell metal $\textcircled{2}$ carbon nanostructures. During the preparation, the key to success was to fabricate core–shell MOF templates and precisely control the pyrolysis parameters.143–148 Qiu et al. demonstrated the preparation of MOF-derived double-shelled nanocages (DSNCs) through the pyrolysis of a core–shell MOF hybrid template.149 In brief, ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanocrystals were employed as seeds for the epitaxial overgrowth of outer ZIF-67(Co) shells to form core–shell ZIF- $\cdot8\textcircled{\mathrm{a}}\mathrm{ZIF-67}$ hybrids.150,151 Then, the MOF hybrids were subjected to annealing under nitrogen at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for carbonization and Zn evaporation, and the resultant powder was washed with diluted $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to remove the unstable Co species. Finally, the MOF-derived DSNCs were obtained featuring a uniform hollow morphology with a ZIF-8(Zn) derived N-decorated microporous carbon and Co-doped N-decorated mesoporous graphitic carbon (Co-NGC) as inner and outer shells, respectively.
Most of the MOFs and MOF-derivatives are restricted in microporous regimes, and hence suffer from high mass diffusion and thereby limited accessibility of active sites. To overcome this drawback, several approaches were proposed to enlarge the porosity and extend the channel or pore volumes, including etching methods, templating methods and defect inducing methods. Accordingly, MOF oriented self-templated pyrolysis is also considered as a facile and effective approach for the in situ generation of defects and vacancies to prepare hierarchically porous nanostructures.152–154 Very recently, Xu and co-workers for the first time reported the fabrication of submillimeter-scaled carbon networks with hierarchical porosity and atomic dispersed metal sites (ADMS) through a facile one-step pyrolysis employing a nitrogen-rich ligand containing MOF as the sacrificial template (Fig. 17).155 In a typical synthesis, Zn-based triazole-rich energetic MOFs doped with Co and/or Fe ions were pre-synthesized as the precursors and pyrolyzed at $1000\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ under an inert atmosphere. During thermolysis, the doped metal precursors were converted into ADMS embedded in the ligand derived carbon networks, and the volatilization and puffing up of Zn nodes and thermally unstable triazole ligands generated a large amount of gases and hence foamed the hierarchically porous carbon network.
After having presented the various MOFs and MOF derived composites that have been employed as catalysts in heterogeneous catalysis especially biomass conversion, the following sections will describe the biomass valorization reactions in which these materials have been used as catalysts, with a special emphasis on commenting their stability and performance in comparison with other types of heterogeneous catalysts.
## 2. Pristine MOF
## 2.1 Transformation of cellulosic biomass
Hydrolysis of biomass to saccharides or platform chemicals is one of the important industrial reactions requiring acid catalysts. In this context, MIL-53(Al) was reported as a heterogeneous solid catalyst for the direct conversion of carboxymethyl cellulose to HMF in the aqueous phase.156 Among the various analogous catalysts tested for this reaction (Table 1), MIL-53(Al) showed $40.3\%$ yield of HMF in water at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . In spite of the lack of coordinatively unsaturated positions around ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ in the ideal MIL-53(Al) structure, ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -TPD analysis showed the presence of relatively weak Brønsted acidic sites $\left(0.38\mathrm{~\mmol~{\g}^{-1}}\right)$ which are responsible for promoting this reaction and are probably associated with structural defects. Measurement of the acidity of solids with ammonia and other basic molecules provides the total number of acid sites responsible for catalytic activity. Further, acid strength distribution can be routinely determined and should linearly correlate with the catalytic activity when these sites are responsible for the reaction. MIL-53(Al) showed similar activity for three cycles without much decrease in its activity. Powder XRD of reused MIL-53(Al) showed no changes in its crystalline pattern compared to the fresh solid. ICP analysis showed no aluminum in the hot filtration test, thus proving the heterogeneity of the catalytic conversion. The superior activity of MIL-53(Al) compared to MIL-101(Cr) is due to the presence of Brønsted acid sites in MIL-53(Al) responsible for the hydrolysis, while such active sites are lacking in MIL-101(Cr). On one hand, this example illustrates the role that the external surface of the MOF particle plays in the hydrolysis of biopolymers and, on the other hand, the importance of lattice imperfections in MOFs in catalysis.
Besides hydrolysis of cellulosic feedstocks, the conversion of sugars to platform molecules is one of the key processes in biomass transformation. In this regard, a series of M-MOF-74 $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni},\mathbf{Mg},\mathbf{Co},\mathbf{Zn}\right)$ solids were prepared and their activity was tested in the conversion of sugars to methyl lactate.157 Among the various catalysts tested, Mg-MOF-74 exhibited the highest yield of methyl lactate from sucrose of $47\%$ , which is even higher than the yield reached from glucose or fructose (Fig. 18). The Mg-MOF-74 catalyst was reusable for three cycles for the conversion of glucose to methyl lactate. However, further work is still necessary to determine the reasons for the higher yield of methyl lactate from sucrose than glucose and to provide characterization data of the spent catalyst. Characterization of the nature of active sites responsible for this reaction is also needed.
## 2.2 Transformation of C5/C6 sugars
One straightforward route for (hemi)cellulose transformation is hydrolysis, followed by subsequent transformation of C5/C6 monosaccharides into platform chemicals. The conversion of glucose to HMF was achieved by employing mixed-metal MIL88B(Fe,Sc) MOFs as heterogeneous solid catalysts to afford $25\%$ yield of HMF at $70\%$ glucose conversion at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DMSO.158 In contrast, MIL-88B(Fe) showed only $3.8\%$ yield of HMF under similar experimental conditions. These differences in the catalytic activity of these two MOFs clearly indicate the enhanced Lewis acid catalysis of mixed-metal MIL-88B(Fe,Sc), as confirmed by direct acidity measurements with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{TPD}$ that showed the presence of around 0.36 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ acid sites. Although the solid was used for three cycles without decay, a sudden drop in its activity was observed in the fourth use (Fig. 19), suggesting catalyst deactivation by the formation of unwanted byproducts. Although the powder XRD pattern of MIL-88B(Fe,Sc) is identical to that of MIL-88B, upon prolonged heating in DMSO, a peak broadening without the appearance of new diffraction peaks was observed. These changes probably indicate some structural deterioration occurring during the treatment.
A hydrothermally stable Yb-based MOF with a molecular formula of $\mathrm{Yb}_{6}(\mathrm{BDC})_{7}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}$ was synthesized and its activity was reported for the conversion of glucose to HMF in water.159 Structural analysis of this MOF indicates that it consists of both bridging hydroxyls and metal coordinated water molecules, thus, the metal nodes can behave as Brønsted and Lewis acid sites, respectively. In agreement, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -TPD analysis indicated the presence of Brønsted and Lewis acid sites. This Yb-based MOF afforded $25\%$ conversion of glucose with $16\%$ HMF yield at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in water. The Yb-based solid MOF exhibited similar conversion and selectivity for four cycles, evidencing its stability under reaction conditions. Although SEM images revealed some minor changes in the morphology of the reused solid with respect to the fresh catalyst, powder XRD showed intact crystallinity.
Isomerization of glucose to fructose without the formation of any undesired by-product is one of the key transformations in biomass valorization, since it is generally assumed that HMF is formed from fructose dehydration.160 In this context, MIL-101(Cr) was functionalized with a series of functional groups in the linker $\left(\mathrm{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2},\mathbf{\Gamma}-\left(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{3}\right)_{2},\mathbf{\Gamma}-\mathbf{NO}_{2},\mathbf{\Gamma}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}\right)$ and their activity was examined in the isomerization of glucose to fructose in aqueous media. Introduction of functional groups in the linkers is one of the effective strategies to modulate the Lewis acid strength around the $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites by inductive effects of the organic linker on the metal nodes. Among the various catalysts screened for this isomerization reaction, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ exhibited high conversion of glucose $(78\%)$ and complete selectivity to fructose at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in water. The catalyst was reused for three cycles without diminishing conversion and selectivity. The hot filtration test confirmed heterogeneity of the reaction. Besides isomerization of glucose to fructose, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ also promoted the direct conversion of amylase with yields of 5.3, 0.8, and $9.6\%$ for glucose, fructose, and cellobiose, respectively, at $\mathrm{pH}4.$ These catalytic data indicate that $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ can effectively be used for the transformation of sugars under acidic conditions.
The nature of active sites and the local environment of these active sites in the framework are important parameters determining the reaction rate and product selectivity. In one of the examples illustrating the influence of these factors, MIL-101(Cr) possessing exclusively $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ as Lewis acid sites promotes glucose-to-fructose isomerization either in water or methanol as solvents at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .161 Further, MIL-101(Cr) favored epimerization of glucose to mannose without undergoing intramolecular carbon shift at elevated temperature. In contrast, Lewis acid $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ sites in UiO-66 in aqueous media showed simultaneously activity for glucose isomerization and epimerization and interestingly, the glucose-to-mannose epimerization occurred through an intramolecular carbon skeleton rearrangement (Fig. 20). To understand the different behavior of MIL-101(Cr) and UiO-66(Zr), it has been proposed that the ionic radius of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ is larger than that of the Cr ion leading to a higher polarizability for $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ sites and with an increased electronegativity for adjacent oxygen atoms. Thus, $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ favors the formation of an eightcoordinate mononuclear complex inducing epimerization together with the cleavage of the C-2 and C-3 bonds and formation of a new bond between C-1 and C-3. On other hand, two $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites interact with glucose to form a hexa-coordinate binuclear complex along with lower polarizability and steric hindrance in trimeric octahedral clusters, thus resulting predominantly in glucose isomerization without the carbonskeleton rearrangement (Fig. 20).
## 2.3 Transformation of platform molecules
As commented in the introduction, biomass transformation processes are well suited to develop cascade and tandem reactions as the conversion of saccharides into 2,5-disubstitured furans. A one-pot tandem reaction has been reported for the coupling of HMF oxidation to 2,5-diformylfuran (DFF) which subsequently undergoes Knoevenagel condensation with active methylene compounds to afford furilydenepropanenitrile derivatives that can be used as monomers for polymers. Hence, a series of MOFs such as MIL-100(Fe), Fe(BTC), $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ treated MIL-101(Fe) was tested for this one-pot reaction at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in acetonitrile (Scheme 1).162 The observed catalytic data indicate that $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}F}\cdot$ -treated MIL-100(Fe) exhibited $100\%$ HMF conversion with complete selectivity to DFF. The superior activity of this catalyst was proposed to be due to the easy accessibility of the reactants to the Fe sites due to comparatively higher surface area and pore volume than the other materials. The second step was successfully achieved under similar conditions with high selectivity towards the desired product. Although the solid was used for three cycles, XRD of the used solid indicated some change in its crystallinity with respect to the fresh catalyst. Further, BET measurements of the used catalyst showed decreased surface area compared to the fresh solid, thus suggesting the presence of adsorbed organic products within the pores probably blocking some sites with respect to the fresh material. This surface area decrease can explain the decay in its activity during the reusability test.
UiO-66 frameworks are built by 12-coordination around the metal node, while MOF-808 presents a 6-coordination environment with coordinatively exchangeable centers available for catalytic activity (Fig. 21). The catalytic performance of these solids was examined in the Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley (MPV) reduction of furfural using isopropanol as a reducing agent. The highest activity under the optimized reaction conditions was achieved with MOF-808 based on Hf and Zr solids. Thus, Hf-MOF-808 showed two-fold enhancement in the reaction rate with respect to Zr-MOF-808 (Fig. 22).163 Further, UiO-66(Hf) and UiO- $66\substack{-\textnormal{N H}_{2}}$ (Hf) also exhibited significant activity to give $80\%$ yield of furfuryl alcohol. Under similar conditions, UiO-66(Zr) showed moderate activity. Later, the activity of Hf-MOF-808 was tested in the MPV reduction of ethyl levulinate to $\gamma$ -valerolactone (GVL), reaching $94\%$ yield of the desired product without formation of by-products at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 23). In contrast, Zr-MOF-808 under identical conditions showed three times lower reaction rate to GVL. A similar trend was also observed for the MPV reduction of furfural and HMF with these catalysts. Hf-MOF808 was used for five cycles with a minor decrease in activity and BET measurements of the used catalyst showed some decrease in the surface area due to product adsorption. The five times used Hf-MOF-808 was characterized by FT-IR spectroscopy, XRD, TGA, and ICP analyses, indicating the high stability of this catalyst under these conditions. Interestingly, the use of Hf-MOF-808 with the Brønsted acidic Al-beta zeolite afforded $75\%$ yield of GVL from furfural as a one-pot transformation. Overall, this comparative study between the catalytic activity of UiO-66 and MOF-808 illustrates the versatility that MOFs offer in catalysis to control the intrinsic activity of acid centers depending on the composition, structure of the MOF and ligand substitution.
## 3. Functionalized MOFs
## 3.1 Transformation of cellulosic biomass
Table 2 gathers the existing reports describing the catalytic activity of modified MOFs as catalysts for cellulose transformation, indicating the MOF used in the process, the main product, its yield and stability data.
In one seminal contribution, Kitagawa and co-workers reported the preparation of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ by a solvothermal reaction of chromium(VI) oxide and monosodium 2-sulfoterephthalic acid (MSBDC) in the presence of hydrochloric acid in water. The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ was studied in the hydrolysis of cellulose at $120{^\circ\mathbf C}$ .164 No hydrolysis of cellulose was observed in the absence of the catalyst, while MIL-101(Cr)
showed $0.2\%$ of xylose and glucose under similar conditions. Interestingly, illustrating the influence of the sulfonic acid group, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ efficiently promoted the hydrolysis of cellulose to afford 2.6, 1.4, and $1.2\%$ yields of xylose, glucose, and cellobiose, respectively. $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR spectroscopy analysis indicated the absence of HMF, levulinic acid, and formic acid which are the most common undesired by-products observed in this reaction. No decrease in the activity of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ even after thirteen uses was observed. In comparison, Amberlyst-15 gave a glucose yield of $4.8\%$ in the first run, but its performance was degraded to less than $1\%$ after ten cycles. These catalytic data clearly establish the superior stability of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ compared to the commercial catalyst under these conditions.
Besides playing an active role as catalysts, MOFs can also be employed as hosts to encapsulate enzymes. In one of these examples, $\upbeta$ -glucosidase $\left(\beta{\-}\mathbf{G}\right)$ has been encapsulated within Cu–PABA (PABA: p-aminobenzoic acid) $\left[\beta{\mathsf{{\mathrm{-G}}@C u-(P A B A)}}\right]$ by coprecipitation as shown in Fig. 24.165 Considering the relative dimensions of $\upbeta\cdot$ -G and the pores of Cu–(PABA), it is likely that the isolated protein molecules should be surrounded by a certain Cu–(PABA) shell. The experimental evidence showed that $\upbeta$ -G@Cu–(PABA) exhibited high resistance to acids and heating and also higher activity in the hydrolysis of carboxymethyl cellulose. For instance, the mixture of $\scriptstyle{\beta\cdot{\bf G}\ @{\bf C}{\bf u}}$ (PABA) biocomposite and co-immobilized cellulase gave $98\%$ yield of glucose which is two-fold higher activity than that observed with co-immobilized cellulase alone. The $\beta{\mathsf{{\mathrm{-G}}@{\mathsf{C u}}(P A B A)}}$ biocomposite was recycled for eight cycles with $70\%$ productivity. These results are highly encouraging considering that the activity of the encapsulated enzyme is retained upon repeated cycling in a reaction where many unfavorable products are formed. However, the characterization data of spent catalyst are still lacking and the reasons for catalyst deactivation have not been addressed.
Similarly to the case of $\mathsf{\beta{\mathrm{-}}G@C u(P A B A)}$ , cellulase has also been supported/encapsulated on $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to afford cellula$\mathrm{se(\bar{a})U i O}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The experimental data indicated improved thermostability, pH tolerance and lifetime for cellulase $\textcircled{2}$ UiO$66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{H}}_{2}$ .166 The activity of cellulase $\mathbf{\Theta}(\mathbf{\bar{a}})\mathbf{UiO-}66\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ was tested in the hydrolysis of carboxymethyl cellulose to glucose in the temperature range of $30\mathrm{-}80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathsf{p H}$ range of 3–7. The superior catalytic activity of this composite was retained even after ten cycles or up to 30 days of storage. Although the stabilization of cellulase over MOF is studied in terms of thermal stability, no activity data regarding the yield of glucose and other products observed in this reaction are discussed. Thus, it appears that stability gain is a general trait in enzyme– MOF composites. Since enzymes are an important class of catalysts in biomass conversion, it seems that there is still much room for further exploration of the role of MOFs to achieve higher activity.
## 3.2 Transformation of C5/C6 sugars
Table 3 collects the modified MOFs that have been used for the transformation of C5/C6 saccharides as well as the yields that have been achieved and the evidence provided in support of catalyst stability.
A new hybrid material consisting of mesoporous tin phosphate (SnP) encapsulated within the pores of robust MIL-101(Cr) $\mathrm{\Delta[SnP@MIL-101(Cr)]}$ has been reported as a bifunctional Brønsted and Lewis acid catalyst in the dehydration of xylose to furfural.167 TEM images revealed the existence of mesoporous SnP particles of $1\mathrm{nm}$ diameter within the cubic MIL-101(Cr) crystals with an edge dimension of ${\sim}200{\sim}300~\mathrm{nm}$ . This catalyst offers Brønsted acidity from hydrogen phosphate groups, while Cr and Sn atoms behave as Lewis acid sites for the dehydration of xylose. The catalytic data showed a promising performance of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{SnP}\ @\mathbf{MIL-}101(\mathbf{Cr}),}\end{array}$ , providing $86.7\%$ furfural yield at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Further, the composite maintained its activity for ten cycles, while in contrast MIL-101(Cr) significantly lost its activity after four cycles.
In another precedent, the hydrothermal stability of MIL-101(Cr) was enhanced by functionalization of the external surface of the crystallites with hydrophobic octadecyltrichlorosilane (OTS) without compromising its internal surface area (mostly of the total area) and porosity.168 The surface functionalization of MIL-101(Cr) by OTS was ascertained by XPS analysis. Among the various loadings of OTS, MIL-101(Cr)-OTS-0.5 (0.5 mmol OTS per g of MIL-101(Cr)) exhibited $56\%$ yield of furfural from xylose and its durability was improved to eight cycles (Fig. 25). This superior activity of MIL-101(Cr)-OTS compared to the pristine MOF was due to the protection of the active sites by the hydrophobic silane groups.
In an alternative strategy, the hydrothermal stability of MIL-101(Cr) was improved by forming a composite between industrial fly ash and MIL-101(Cr). This composite showed a furfural yield and selectivity of $71\%$ and $80\%$ , respectively, and was much superior to the pristine MIL-101(Cr) catalyst showing $46\%$ yield under identical conditions.169 Further, the activity of the composite was retained for ten cycles without any significant activity decay, while MIL-101(Cr) exhibited activity up to four cycles. The synergism of this composite was attributed to the dual contribution from nodal Cr atoms of MIL-101(Cr) and hydroxyl groups of activated fly ash behaving as Lewis and Brønsted acid sites, respectively.
In another report, MIL-101(Cr)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ was used as a heterogeneous solid catalyst for the production of furfural from xylose in a biphasic cyclopentyl methyl ether $\mathrm{(CPME)/H_{2}O-N a C}$ l solvent (Fig. 26).170 Under optimized reaction conditions, a maximum yield of $70.8\%$ furfural at $97.8\%$ xylose conversion was achieved at $170^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . In contrast, MCM-41 has been reported to convert xylose into furfural at $96.85\%$ xylose conversion with $44\%$ furfural yield at $170\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.^{171}$ The observed activity with $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ for the production of furfural was significantly higher than that of MCM-41. This improved activity was attributed to the higher surface area, abundant Lewis and Brønsted acid sites of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ in comparison to MCM-41. The catalyst was reused for five cycles with a minimal decrease in its activity due to the formation of oligomeric products that remain adsorbed on MIL-101(Cr)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ . However, powder XRD and FT-IR spectroscopy showed that the characteristic peaks of the fresh MIL-101(Cr)- $S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ catalyst are maintained upon reuse.
A Sn-carboxylate MOF, namely, UZAR-S10 (UZAR: Universidad de Zaragoza), was obtained from $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ ions and 2-aminobenzene1,4-dicarboxylate as a ligand and its activity was examined in the conversion of sucrose to methyl levulinate.172 Powder XRD revealed the crystalline nature of this MOF. The catalytic activity of UZAR-S10 in the production of methyl levulinate was also compared with an analogous MOF in which $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ ions were substituted into the $\mathrm{Ti}_{12}$ cluster SBU of MIP-177-LT (MIP-177- $\mathrm{LT}\mathrm{(Ti/Sn)})$ (MIP: Materials from the Institute of Porous Materials from Paris; LT: low temperature). The highest methyl levulinate yield from sucrose was $29.5\%$ and $42\%$ for UZAR-S10 and MIP-177-LT(Ti/Sn), respectively. This enhanced performance of $\mathbf{MIP-177-LT(Ti/Sn)}$ compared to UZAR-S10 was attributed to the facile diffusion of sugar through the micropores of MIP-177- $\mathrm{LT(Ti/Sn)}$ . Nevertheless, the activity of UZAR-S10 was much superior to that of other carboxylate MOFs like MIL-53(Al), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53\mathrm{(Al)}$ , UiO-66 $(\mathrm{zr})$ , MIL-101(Cr) and HKUST-1(Cu) under similar conditions. Although $\mathbf{MIP-177-LT(Ti/Sn)}$ was used for five cycles with no appreciable decay in its activity and crystallinity, a significant decrease in BET surface area from 466 to $85{-}112~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after the first cycle was observed and attributed to the strong adsorption of some products.
UiO-66(Zr) is one among the structurally more robust MOFs for heterogeneous catalysis.172 Taking advantages of this feature, a highly stable UiO-66 has been reported as a heterogeneous catalyst for the conversion of glucose $(16\%)$ into HMF with $10\%$ yield at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in water.173 In contrast, the yield of HMF was enhanced to $28\%$ at $31\%$ glucose conversion by employing UiO $\mathord{\left.\leftrightharpoons}66-\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}\right.$ as a solid catalyst (Fig. 27). The enhanced activity of UiO- ${\cdot66{\cdot}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ was due to the presence of Brønsted acidic sites in combination with the Lewis sites in the parent MOF. This activity achieved with UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ is similar to that reported for Sn-beta zeolite,174 which is one of the most active solid catalysts for this process suggesting the comparable activity between MOFs and zeolites. Reusability test using the UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}(20)$ (the number in brackets indicates the percentage of $\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ groups) catalyst revealed a small decrease in glucose conversion after the first run, but no further decay in activity was observed in the next three recycles. This behavior suggests that after the first use the UiO-6 $\mathbf{\partial}_{\left\mathrm{6-SO_{3}H}\left(20\right)}$ sample has reached a stationary catalytic activity.
The influence of the sulfonic acid groups in glucose to HMF transformation needs, however, to be further addressed with the specific roles of sulfonic groups and mechanistic details. This interest derives from another report in which the activity of UiO-66 was compared with its functionalized analogues such as UiO- $66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ catalysts in the conversion of glucose to HMF, but opposite conclusions were reached.175 The maximum yield of HMF $(28\%)$ was attained with UiO-66 in DMSO at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the yield was further enhanced to $37\%$ in a water/DMSO mixture under similar conditions. On other hand, the activity of UiO- $66\substack{-\textnormal{N H}_{2}}$ was $4\%$ which is lower than UiO-66 and only $5\%$ yield was observed with $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ as a catalyst (Fig. 28) under similar conditions. The decreased activity of the functionalized UiO-66 MOFs compared to the parent UiO-66 was proposed to reflect the decrease of surface area upon accommodation of $\boldsymbol{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups within the micropores. However, this observation requires further studies, particularly considering the influence of the substituent on the linker on the catalytic activity of the metal nodes and the Brønsted acidity of sulfonic acid groups. UiO-66 retained its activity for five cycles without any notable decay in its activity.
The MOF-808 structure is constituted by the assembly between octahedral $\mathrm{\Gamma[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}]^{12+}}$ cationic nodes and BTC as linkers. The secondary building units are connected by six BTC linkers and six formate ligands cap the node for charge compensation. Furthermore, MOF-808 has a larger internal pore diameter of $18.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ compared to $6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ for UiO-66. Interestingly, the catalytic activity of MOF-808 was also tested for the conversion of glucose to HMF under identical conditions, observing $31\%$ yield, which is higher than UiO-66. This superior performance of MOF-808 was ascribed to the high surface area $\left(1970{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}\right)$ and internal pore diameter of MOF-808 with respect to UiO-66, thus favouring reactants to more easily access the $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ centres without much diffusion limitations.
The cooperation between acid and basic sites in solid catalysts for glucose transformation has been claimed. According to this report, isomerization of glucose to fructose would involve the action of basic sites, while the dehydration of fructose to HMF would require acidic sites.176 In this context, recently, PU foam was modified by PDA and used as a carrier to prepare multifunctional MOF membranes through the assembly of zirconium nodes by MSBDC and 2-aminoterephthalic acid linkers to obtain a mixed linker MOF supported on PDA/PU, UiO-6 $\mathrm{6{-}S O_{3}H\mathrm{-}N H_{2}/}$ PDA@PU.177 Under the optimized reaction conditions, UiO-66- $\mathrm{SO_{3}H\mathrm{-NH_{2}/P D A@P U}}$ afforded $70\%$ HMF yield from glucose at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DMSO. This yield is higher than the value obtained with [ $\mathrm{JiO-66-SO_{3}H/P D A@P U}\left(56.6\%\right)$ under identical conditions. These catalytic data clearly suggest the involvement of bifunctional acid/base sites in promoting the conversion of glucose to HMF. The catalytic activity of the acid/base bifunctional MOF was maintained up to five cycles with no decay in its activity.
In the search for water-tolerant Lewis acids to be employed for the formation of HMF from glucose in aqueous media, Hara and co-workers reported that modification of bulk $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by phosphate groups increases remarkably the activity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in aqueous media.178 In this way, $\mathrm{PO_{4}/T i O_{2}}$ is a benchmark catalyst for fructose conversion to HMF. Inspired by these precedents, partial phosphate modification of zirconia nodes in an NU-1000 MOF was achieved and the catalytic performance of the phosphate-modified NU-1000 was studied in the conversion of glucose to HMF.179 The NU-1000 MOF is constructed by the assembly of zirconia cluster nodes and pyrene linkers and the isolated metal nodes have been reported as Lewis acid catalysts for the destruction of chemical warfare agents.180,181 A complete phosphate modification of NU-1000 $\left(\mathrm{PO_{4}/N U(e q)}\right)$ resulted in a catalyst exhibiting $5.9\%$ yield of HMF, while partial modification of NU-1000 by phosphate $\left(\mathrm{PO_{4}/N U(h a l f)}\right)$ gave $15\%$ yield of HMF in water at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A maximum yield of HMF $(24\%)$ was observed under identical conditions using $\mathrm{PO}_{4}/$ NU(half) as the catalyst in a water/2-PrOH mixture. The lower catalytic activity of $\mathrm{PO_{4}/N U(e q)}$ indicated that the full-coverage of phosphate over NU-1000 poisoned the catalytic activity as shown in Scheme 2. The efficiency of metal node modification was confirmed by synthesising phosphate-modified bulk $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ catalysts at similar phosphate loadings applied for NU-1000. The observed catalytic data clearly indicated that $\mathrm{PO_{4}/N U(h a l f)}$ exhibited $64\%$ yield of HMF, while all other bulk zirconia and modified catalysts were unselective to produce fructose or HMF, while exhibiting significant glucose consumption (Fig. 29).
Janiak and co-workers have reported the catalytic activity of a series of substituted MIL-101(Cr)-X (X: H, $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ ) in the conversion of glucose to HMF.182 MIL-101(Cr)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ afforded $29\%$ yield of HMF in a $\mathrm{THF}{:}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ mixture at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while other catalysts like $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101\mathbf{(Cr)-NO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101\mathbf{(Cr)}{-}\mathbf{NO}_{2}/\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ showed around $12{-}13\%$ HMF yield under similar conditions. The pristine MIL-101(Cr) showed $2\%$ HMF yield. However, no product formation was noticed in pure THF with all these catalysts revealing the effective role of water. MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ exhibited preferential selectivity to HMF with respect to levulinic acid (molar ratio $\textstyle1:0.3]$ , while the commercial catalysts like Amberlyst-15 and sulfuric acid provided mostly levulinic acid, with HMF to levulinic acid ratios of 1 : 3 and $1{:}10$ , respectively. In contrast, $\mathbf{MIL}{-}101\mathbf{(Cr)-NO}_{2}$ exclusively gave HMF under similar conditions. Further, a control experiment proved that $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ is incapable of converting HMF to levulinic acid. Although $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ remained porous and crystalline in reusability tests, its deactivation by humin formation could not be prevented. The BET surface area was reduced from $1333\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for the fresh solid to $443~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after the first run, becoming negligible after four runs $(91~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Efforts to reactivate this sample by washing with HCl slightly increased the BET surface area to $140\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , which is far behind the original value. In another precedent, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ was also claimed as a bifunctional solid catalyst promoting the conversion of glucose with $45\%$ yield of HMF.183 This higher HMF yield could reflect the use of optimal experimental conditions. The catalyst maintained its activity for five cycles with no notable decay in its performance. Powder XRD also supported the maintenance of the structural integrity in the reused solid with respect to the fresh solid.
A multifunctional catalyst was designed by loading Pd NPs inside the pores of UiO-66 whose particles are deposited on SGO to obtain a $\mathbf{Pd/UiO-}66@\mathbf{SGO}$ composite. The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pd/UiO-}66@\mathbf{SGO}$ was studied for the one-pot transformation of fructose and glucose to 2,5-dimethylfuran (2,5-DMF).184 This reaction was promoted by the dehydration of fructose using Brønsted acid U $\scriptstyle\operatorname{iO-}66@\mathbf{S}\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}$ while in the presence of hydrogen HMF converted to 2,5-DMF due to the activity of Pd NPs (Scheme 3). A maximum yield from fructose of $70.5\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ 2,5-DMF was achieved using $4.8\ \mathrm{Pd/UiO-}66@\mathrm{SGO}$ (Pd size $3.5\pm$ $0.5\ \mathrm{nm}\cdot$ at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in THF, while no product was detected in controls using GO, SGO, UiO-66 and UiO-66/SGO. The use of $4.8\mathrm{\Pd/SGO}$ afforded $38.5~\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of product under identical conditions. On the other hand, glucose was converted to 2,5-DMF with $45.3\ \mathrm{mol\%}$ yield in the presence of 4.8 Pd/UiO$66@{\bf S G O}$ under identical reaction conditions. This lower activity is caused by the need for an additional isomerization of glucose to fructose in the cascade process. Reusability of the $4.8~\mathrm{Pd},$ /UiO$66@{\bf S G O}$ catalyst shows that the yield of 2,5-DMF decreases in the first two runs to $58.3~\mathrm{\mol\%}$ , but after that the activity is maintained for the three additional cycles at $52.1\mathrm{\mol}\%$ .
This decrease in the activity of the first two cycles was rationalized considering Pd leaching from 4.8 to $4.3~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of fresh and the spent catalyst, respectively. Furthermore, the BET surface area of the spent catalyst showed a decrease from 715 to $635~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while the pore volume decreased from 0.40 to $0.25~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which can also be responsible for some decay in the catalytic activity.
In another study, MIL-100(Fe) was loaded with lysine functionalized phosphotungstic acid (Lys-PM2) and the resulting composite was examined as a heterogeneous solid catalyst for the conversion of glucose to levulinic acid in water (Fig. 30).185 Among the various reaction conditions screened, the highest yield of levulinic acid was achieved to be $57.9\%$ at $150\ {^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}.$ The activity of this composite was attributed to the combination of Brønsted and Lewis acid sites. Although the yield of levulinic acid was $64\%$ employing Cr/H-ZSM-5 at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the MIL-100(Fe) catalyst operated at a lower reaction temperature and employed abundant Fe metal; thus, it was environmentally preferred. On the other hand, the activity of this catalyst was not very far from that of the zeolite catalyst, thus illustrating the efficiency of MOF-based catalyst for the hydrolysis of sugars to platform chemicals. The catalyst was reused for four cycles with a gradual decay in its activity. However, no structural changes were observed for the fresh and reused Lys-PM2@MIL100(Fe) solids by XRD and IR studies.
A series of MOFs like MIL-101(Cr), UiO-66(Zr), MIL-53(Al) were post-synthetically modified with chlorosulfonic acid to obtain the corresponding sulfonated derivatives MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H},$ and $\mathbf{MIL-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and their activity was examined in the conversion of fructose to HMF.186 The Brønsted acidity was found to decrease in the order of $\mathrm{MIL-101(Cr)}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO_{3}H}\left(15.0\%\right)>\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr)}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO_{3}H}\left(9.5\%\right)>\mathrm{MIL}.53(\mathrm{Al})+ $ $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(8.2\%\right)~>$ $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(6.1\%)$ 4 $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(3.0\%\right)$ , thus suggesting the difference in the density of acid sites. The values in parentheses indicate the content of $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups on these solids. $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(15.0\%)$ afforded $90\%$ HMF yield at complete fructose conversion in DMSO at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ On the other hand, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(6.1\%)$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(3.0\%\right)$ showed 71 and $63\%$ yields of HMF, respectively under identical conditions. In contrast, MIL-101(Cr) gave $24\%$ HMF yield at $45\%$ fructose conversion under similar conditions. These catalytic experiments convincingly prove that the yield of HMF in these solids depends on the density of $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. Furthermore, UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(9.5\%\right)$ and $\mathbf{MIL-}53(\mathbf{Al})–\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ $\left(8.2\%\right)$ showed 85 and $79\%$ HMF yields, respectively under identical conditions. The catalytic performance of MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ $\left(15.0\%\right)$ was also compared with a series of zeolites, observing that fructose dehydration at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ decreases in the order of MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ $\left(15.0\%\right)$ $>$ PTA/MIL-101 (PTA: phosphotungstic acid, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ , $33.8\mathrm{\wt\%}$ ) $>$ H-ZSM-5 $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al})=25\$ ) $>$ HY-zeolite $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}~\ge~5.2)~>~\mathrm{H}$ -beta $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}=12.5)$ (Fig. 31). These results indicated that the highest activity in HMF yield was achieved with MIL-101(Cr)- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ $\left(15.0\%\right)$ , while the performance of PTA/ MIL-101 (PTA $33.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) was comparable to HZSM-5 $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}=25)$ and HY-zeolite $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al})\ge5.2\$ . These experimental data suggested that the activity of a solid can be enhanced by increasing the acid strength without compromising the porosity, surface area and three dimensional crystalline structure. $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(15.0\%\right)$ was used for five cycles with no significant decay in its activity and the reaction was heterogeneous in nature. However, BET surface area measurement indicated a significant decay in the surface area from $1757~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the fresh to $1146~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the recovered solid suggesting the adsorption of humins on the catalyst, which is one of the common deactivation pathways in carbohydrate dehydration. Further, the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{\nabla}\times\mathbf{I}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}101\big(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\big)\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ $\left(15.0\%\right)$ was also tested with other saccharides. The observed activity data are summarized in Table 4. As can be seen there, the efficiency of the process decreases considerably for cellulose as the starting material, even using ILs $[(\mathrm{{Bmim})C l]}$ as medium. This observation is somewhat expected considering the diffusion limitations that cellulose should experience to access to the internal active sites within the MOFs.
One of the important advantages of MOFs compared to zeolites is their higher flexibility in the tailoring of the pore diameter and surface area which can accommodate a large number of guests, like metal NPs, drugs, dyes, metalloporphyrin and polyoxometalate clusters.187–189
Two hierarchically porous MOFs, namely, NUS-6 (NUS: National University of Singapore), comprising zirconium or hafnium clusters with UiO-66 topology having stability and strong Brønsted acidity were prepared and used as catalysts in fructose dehydration to HMF.190 The structural analysis revealed that the BET surface areas were 550 and $530\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for NUS-6(Zr) and NUS-6(Hf), respectively, with a unique hierarchical system of micropores $\left(\sim0.5,\sim0.7\right)$ , and $\sim1.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and mesopores $\left(\sim4.0\ \mathrm{\nm}\right)$ with dangling sulfonic acid groups. A complete conversion of fructose was achieved with $98\%$ yield of HMF using NUS-6(Hf) at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while the use of NUS-6(Zr) provided $84\%$ yield under similar conditions. The activity of NUS-6(Hf) was comparatively superior than those of MIL-53(Al)- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(\sim79\%$ yield), UiO $){\displaystyle-66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}}\mathrm{H}$ $(\sim85\%$ yield) and $\mathrm{\bfMIL^{-101}(C r)^{-S O_{3}H}}$ ( $90\%$ yield).191 Although NUS-6(Hf) and NUS- $6(\mathrm{Zr})$ exhibited identical crystal structure, the difference in the activity was due to the additional Brønsted acidity in NUS-6(Hf) caused by polarized $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups in Hf clusters as well as the facile dissociation of acidic protons by oxophilic Hf cations. This was further confirmed by performing additional control experiments with pristine UiO-66(Zr) and UiO-66(Hf) without any Brønsted acid sites, whereby yields of 0 and $8\%$ were measured. These catalytic data revealed the role of Brønsted acid sites in NUS-6(Hf). The acidity of these solids was further verified by measuring proton concentration by acid–base titration. UiO-66(Hf) exhibited five times more acidic protons $\mathrm{60~mmol~mol^{-1}},$ ) than ${\bf U i O-}66({\bf Z r})$ confirming a higher density of Brønsted acid sites for Hf with respect to Zr. Further, the acidic proton concentration of NUS- $6(\mathrm{Zr})$ was $(12.9\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , while NUS-6(Hf) showed even higher acidic proton concentration $(13.9\mathrm{\mol\mol^{-1}}.\$ ), thus explaining the highest yield among the catalysts studied for this reaction. Another important reason for achieving very high yield of HMF with NUS-6(Hf) was the confinement of fructose within smaller pores by preventing isomerization of fructose to other furanoside. NUS-6(Hf) was repeatedly reused without observing a significant decay in its activity for three cycles. Furthermore, reused NUS-6(Hf) exhibited high crystallinity, although porosity was compromised due to the trapping of by-products.
One of the challenging tasks in the design of heterogeneous catalysts is to immobilize acid and redox sites on a single multifunctional catalyst that can promote efficiently tandem reactions. In this context, PMA was encapsulated on MIL101(Cr) (PMA/MIL-101(Cr)) and reported as a bifunctional solid catalyst for the one-pot conversion of fructose to DFF.192 As shown in Scheme 4, PMA/MIL-101(Cr) provided two different active sites, namely, Brønsted acid sites and redox sites, which promote dehydration and aerobic oxidation, respectively. PMA is a well-known redox catalyst due to the ability of Mo atoms to exhibit various oxidation states. Among the various loadings of PMA over MIL-101(Cr), 2 wt% PMA/MIL-101(Cr) afforded $75\%$ yield of DFF in DMSO at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The catalyst was used in five consecutive cycles with no significant decrease in activity. ICP analysis revealed a $9\%$ loss of Mo under the reaction conditions.
In another example, $\mathbf{Pd-V}{\big(}3:2{\big)}\ @\mathbf{ML}101{\big(}\mathbf{Cr}{\big)}$ was prepared by an incipient wetness method from Pd and V precursors in 3 : 2 ratio and subsequent reduction in a stream of $\mathbf{N}_{2}/\mathbf{H}_{2}$ $(4:1,\ \mathrm{v/v})$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\mathrm{~2~h~}}$ . The catalytic performance of $\mathbf{Pd-V}{\big(}3:2{\big)}\ @\mathbf{MIL-}101{\big(}\mathbf{Cr}{\big)}$ was also examined in the direct conversion of fructose to DFF (Scheme 5).193 Among the various samples tested, ${5^{0}}/{_{0}}\mathrm{Pd}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{V}{\left(3:2\right)}\ @\mathrm{MIL101}{\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)}$ was found to be the most efficient catalyst affording $34\%$ DFF yield at complete fructose conversion at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, the study should also have addressed other issues such as metal leaching, catalyst reusability and characterization of the spent catalyst.
Tsapatsis and co-workers have reported that the activity of UiO-66 may be enhanced in the isomerization of glucose to fructose by generating defects in the structure during its synthesis using Fm as a modulator. The catalytic data indicated that the activity of UiO-66-Fm was much superior to that of UiO-66 (without any modulator).194 For instance, a fructose yield of $56\%$ was achieved at $82\%$ glucose conversion in 1-propanol. The superior performance of UiO-66-Fm in 1-propanol was believed to be due to the synergism between Lewis (coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ ions) and Brønsted (bridged OH group) sites. This rationalization is in agreement with a similar proposal to justify the activity of commercial zeolites for an identical reaction.195 Further, the activity of UiO-66-Fm is comparable to that of Sn-SPP zeolite in ethanol196 and also similar to UiO-66- $\mathrm{so}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ in water,197 but higher than $\mathbf{NU-1000^{198}}$ . The superior activity of UiO-66-Fm compared to the defect-free UiO-66 could also be due to the availability of larger pores in the defective catalyst which should facilitate the facile diffusion of glucose to reach the active sites. On other hand, the observed catalytic data suggest that the reaction rate mainly depends on the nature of alcohol. The formation of fructose was suppressed completely in methanol, while the highest yield was observed in 1-propanol. Adsorption studies proved that the use of 1-propanol favours the weak interactions of glucose and fructose with the active sites in UiO-66-Fm, but in methanol adsorption is much stronger, thus blocking the active sites. The reaction was heterogeneous and the catalyst maintained its activity for three cycles.
MIL-101(Cr) was prepared employing glycine amino acid (GLY) and the resulting product contained co-precipitated $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ NPs within MIL-101 framework (MIL-101-Gly). The activity of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was tested in the isomerization of glucose to fructose.199 $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly showed identical powder XRD pattern to other MIL-101(Cr) solids prepared by conventional methods. A fructose yield of $59.3\%$ was measured for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly in ethanol at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In comparison, the fructose yields were 20.2, 24.3 and $24.9\%$ for MIL-101(Cr)-HF, MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr)-nano solids, respectively, under similar conditions. Interestingly, the profile of fructose yield with respect to glucose conversion using $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly was very similar to those of Sn-containing zeolites that are the benchmark catalysts for this reaction. For instance, $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly and two Sn zeolites like Sn-MWW and Sn-SPP exhibited 1.2, 0.91, and 0.71 gfructose gcatalyst1, respectively. These experimental results clearly prove that MOFs can afford comparable activity to that of Sn zeolites in carbohydrate isomerization. Reusability of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly showed an initial loss in activity after the first cycle, followed by minor decreases up to four cycles with stable fructose selectivity. XRD patterns and TEM images of the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ -Gly after the fourth run were identical to those of the fresh catalyst.
## 3.3 Transformation of platform molecules
The catalytic activity of Fe(BTC) and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ has been tested in the aldol condensation of furfural with acetone (Scheme 6) to obtain the desired condensation product.204 Although $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{BTC}\right)$ and $\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{BTC})_{2}$ afforded similar selectivity $(70\%)$ of the expected product, the conversion of furfural was 26.2 and 2.7 for Fe(BTC) and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}$ , respectively, under similar conditions. This activity difference between these well-known Lewis acid catalysts suggested that the one possessing exclusively Lewis acid sites showed lower activity in the aldol condensation. On the other hand, the superior activity of Fe(BTC) was attributed to the presence of (weak) Brønsted acid sites in addition to Lewis acid sites arising from the metal centres. These Brønsted acid sites correspond to structural defects, or are due to the polarizing effect of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations promoting the hydrolysis of coordinated water molecules. This notable difference in the activity of $\mathrm{Fe(BTC)}$ in condensation reactions is in close agreement with the difference in activity of Fe(BTC) and MIL-100(Fe) in some selected oxidation reactions.205
$\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ has been reported as a bifunctional Brønsted and Lewis solid catalyst promoting the conversion of levulinic acid and its esters to GVL through a catalytic transfer hydrogenation (CTH) process (Fig. 32).200 The conversion of methyl levulinate to GVL in 2-BuOH as a hydrogen donor using pristine UiO-66 showed $70\%$ conversion with $36\%$ GVL yield at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ In comparison, MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-53(Al) solids did not provide any conversion under similar conditions. On the other hand, commercial $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ exhibited $13\%$ conversion with $6\%$ yield of GVL under identical conditions. These catalytic data revealed that $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ ions present in the framework of UiO-66 are the real active sites in this reaction. In contrast, the selectivity and yield towards GVL were enhanced in the order of UiO-66 $<$ $\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{20}$ $\prec$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{40}$ $\prec$ UiO-66- $\mathrm{SO_{3}H_{100}}~<~\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SO_{3}H_{80}}~<~\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SO_{3}H_{60}}$ . The maximum GVL yield was observed with Ui $0{-}66{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{60}$ ( $60\%$ MSBDC) at complete methyl levulinate conversion reaching $80\%$ GVL yield under the same conditions. These catalytic results along with other control experiments indicate that the superior activity of UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{60}$ originates mainly from the optimal balance among spatial isolation of Zr-oxo Lewis basic centers, the activity of sulfonic acid groups in a confined space, the compromise between empty pore volume for substrate diffusion and sufficient concentration of $\mathrm{so}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. UiO-66- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{60}$ was also employed as a catalyst to convert other levulinate derivatives such as ethyl levulinate, $n$ -butyl levulinate and levulinic acid at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford 45, 31 and $25\%$ yields, respectively. Furthermore, it was observed that the reaction rate decreased upon increasing the chain length of the alkyl moiety in the levulinate esters, a fact that was attributed to steric hindrance due to the alkyl groups (Fig. 33). The activity of UiO-66 ${\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{}S}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{60}$ was maintained for four cycles with no noticeable decrease in its activity.
Design of hybrid catalysts containing MOFs as one of the components is an effective strategy to achieve better activity and selectivity due to the availability of well-defined pore channels in MOFs. In the reported examples, metal oxides and zeolites have been coated with MOF shells and the resulting composite exhibits enhanced catalytic performance. In one of these studies, a $\mathrm{Pt/CeO_{2}@U i O-66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ hybrid catalyst was prepared by modifying the $\mathbf{Pt/CeO}_{2}$ core with polystyrenesulfonate, followed by assembling UiO- ${\cdot66\ –\bf N}{\bf H}_{2}$ through the consecutive addition of metal ions and linkers as shown in Fig. 34.201 The catalytic activity of $\mathbf{Pt-CeO}_{2}@\mathbf{UiO-}66\mathbf{-}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ was examined in the selective hydrogenation of furfural. The as-prepared $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}C e O_{2}@U i O\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ resulted in quantitative conversion of furfural with $>99\%$ selectivity to furfuryl alcohol at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . On the other hand, $\mathbf{Pt-CeO}_{2}$ afforded $89.7\%$ selectivity to furfuryl alcohol initially, but this selectivity value continuously decreased with furfural conversion due to consecutive hydrogenation of furfuryl alcohol to tetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol, 1,2-pentanediol and 1,5-pentanediol. This distinct activity between $\mathbf{Pt}\mathrm{-CeO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}C e O_{2}@U i O\mathrm{-}66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ clearly suggests the important role played by the outer MOF shell in $\mathbf{Pt-CeO}_{2}@$ UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ to achieve chemoselectivity in furfural hydrogenation. The catalyst was used five times with no appreciable change in its activity. In addition, no changes between the fresh and the spent catalyst were seen in morphology, structure and elemental analysis. Furthermore, the BET surface area of the fresh and the recovered catalyst showed similar values, thus suggesting catalyst stability.
In another example of the preparation of multicomponent catalysts, the composite of hierarchical faujasite nanosheets with ZIF-8 (Hie-FAU-ZIF-8) was prepared via a stepwise deposition of ZIF-8 onto the zeolite surface (Fig. 35) that was previously modified to enhance MOF content to increase the hydrophobicity of the material.202 The catalytic activity of the FAU/ZIF-8 composite was studied in the aldol condensation of HMF with acetone to give 4-[5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl]but-3-en-2-one (HMB). TPD analysis of this composite indicated that the acid–base properties of the composite have improved significantly with respect to the individual components. The yield of HMB at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ increased from 16 to $40\%$ with Hie-FAU-ZIF-8-5C (5C indicates the number of cycles employed to form the MOF shell) compared with bare hierarchical FAU or the organic amine-grafted hierarchical FAU. In contrast, the physical mixture of hierarchical FAU and ZIF-8 under a similar weight ratio of Hie-FAU-ZIF-8-5C showed $16\%$ yield of HMB. Among the various catalysts, Hie-FAU-ZIF-8-15C afforded $67\%$ yield of HMB under identical conditions. The superior activity of the composite compared to its individual components is due to the easy accessibility of alkalineexchanged FAU sites, together with unsaturated $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ species in ZIF-8 as acid sites and N-moieties as the basic active sites, thus generating cooperative acid/base activity (Fig. 36). In addition, it was proposed that the superior activity of the composite is also due to the occurrence of synergistic effects between the facile activation of acetone by the base and acid sites to form enolate anions and enol, respectively, and the stabilization of the carbonyl species by the zeolite framework facilitating the nucleophilic attack to give the desired product. The catalyst stability was assessed by reusability tests, observing similar activity for three cycles. However, characterization data of the spent catalyst were not provided and further insights onto catalyst stability are still necessary.
Zeolite beta and UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ MOFs were integrated to obtain a nanocomposite catalyst (Fig. 37) that was used for the synthesis of 2-((5-(hydroxymethyl)furan-2-yl)methylene)malononitrile (HFMM) from sucrose/fructose/glucose through a cascade reaction pathway.203 Under the optimized reaction conditions, the use of beta $(10\%)$ -UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ resulted in $72\%$ , $65\%$ , and $60\%$ yields of HFMM product from fructose, sucrose and glucose, respectively. Control experiments revealed that beta zeolite promoted the transformation of sucrose to HMF, while UiO- $66\substack{-\textnormal{N H}_{2}}$ favours the Knoevenagel condensation reaction. These contrasting results illustrate the advantage of combining the two components to obtain a composite of beta-UiO- $66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ . Furthermore, the beta $(10\%)$ -UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst attained yields of 65%, 63.5%, 61.9%, 60.8%, and 60.3% after the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, $4^{\mathrm{th}}$ and $5^{\mathrm{th}}$ recycles, respectively, indicating a minor loss in the activity after five cycles. XRD, FT-IR, TGA and textural properties of the recycled catalyst were identical to those of the fresh catalyst. Considering the reported data for MOF-based composites, it appears that this type of material is very promising for the development of highly active catalysts in biomass transformation, especially for catalysis of tandem reactions.
## 4. Guests and MOF compounds
This section describes those reports in which MOFs contain guest species with catalytic activity to promote biomass conversion reactions. Table 5 lists the guest containing MOFs and the reactions that have been carried out with an indication of the product yields.
## 4.1 Transformation of cellulosic biomass
Cai and co-workers reported a supported bimetallic $\mathrm{Pd_{1}N i_{4}/}$ MIL-100(Fe) catalyst for the cleavage of lignin to synthesize acetophenone and phenol.206 A complete conversion and $95\%$ total selectivity were achieved under a $130~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , argon atmosphere within $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The experimental results indicated that the remarkably enhanced catalytic activity was related to the synergistic effect between Pd and Ni species and the cooperation of Lewis acid sites of the MIL-100(Fe) support. Additionally, benefiting from the porous structure and structural stability of MIL-100(Fe) under the reaction conditions, the $\mathrm{Pd_{1}N i_{4}/}$ MIL-100(Fe) catalyst showed high stability without any obvious activity loss in five runs.
For the direct transformation of cellulose into ethylene glycol, Chen et al. devised a system composed of a multifunctional catalyst namely Ru/NENU-3, which was synthesized by immobilization of Ru NPs on the hybrid of PTA and HKUST1(Cu) (Fig. 38).207 The incorporated PTA accelerated both cellulose hydrolysis and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ bond cleavage of the generated sugar intermediates for the production of glycolaldehyde. Afterwards, the resultant glycolaldehyde was hydrogenated over the highly dispersed small Ru NPs $(\sim2\ \mathrm{nm})$ to yield the target product ethylene glycol. As a result, a $50.2\%$ yield of ethylene glycol was achieved over $\mathbf{Ru}/\mathbf{NENU}{-3}$ ( $2.4\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ Ru, $34.8\mathrm{\wt\%}$ PTA) under the optimized reaction conditions. The same group also demonstrated the activity of another bifunctional Ru catalyst using the PTA/MOF hybrid as a support for the upgrading of cellulose and cellobiose into sorbitol.208 The synthesis consisted in the one-pot immobilization of PTA into MIL-100(Cr) cavities to prepare the PTA/MOF hybrid, followed by an impregnation process of Ru NPs (Fig. 38). The as-prepared Ru–PTA/MIL-100(Cr) contains abundant Brønsted and Lewis acid sites and metallic Ru sites, thereby exhibiting excellent catalytic performance in the cascade hydrolysis and hydrogenation reactions, affording up to $95.1\%$ and $57.9\%$ sorbitol yields from cellobiose and cellulose, respectively. Noteworthily, this strategy was proven to be applicable for the preparation of a series of different M-PTA/ MOF $[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Pt}]$ Pd and Rh) composites by simply changing the metal precursors.
## 4.2 Transformation of C5/C6 sugars and lignin-derived linkages
A series of dual acid functionalized catalysts, namely, MP-1, MP-2 and MP-3, were developed by Lam and co-workers for the upgrading of xylose to prepare furfural.209 The catalysts were prepared through the one-pot incorporation of SnP into the cavities of an MIL-101(Cr) matrix; accordingly, the Brønsted and Lewis acidity of the as-prepared nanocomposites could be facilely adjusted by tailoring the loadings of phosphate groups and metal species (Cr and Sn atoms), respectively. The MP-2 catalyst with modest acid density (0.225 and $0.402\ \mathrm{mmol}$ pyridine $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ catalyst for Brønsted and Lewis acidity, respectively) exhibited the best catalytic performance in the subsequent xylose transformation, achieving an up to $86.7\%$ furfural yield under the optimal conditions. Additionally, the incorporation of SnP largely promoted the stability and recyclability of the catalytic system. The integrity of the MP-2 catalyst was well-retained under vigorous reaction conditions (e.g. $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ ) after ten cycles without activity loss. In contrast, the pristine MIL-101(Cr) decomposed within four cycles.
Hensen and co-workers reported the one-pot preparation of Brønsted acidic PTA/MIL-101(Cr) catalyst for the selective dehydration of fructose and glucose to yield HMF.210 In brief, the Cr precursors, BDC, hydrofluoric acid and a certain amount of PTA $\left(0.5{-}3.0\ \mathrm{~g}\right)$ were dissolved in water to form a basic aqueous solution (initial $\mathrm{pH}=1$ , modified by NaOH), which was subsequently subjected to a solvothermal process for the selfassembling of MIL-101(Cr) and in situ encapsulation of PTA. The resultant PTA/MIL-101(Cr) was employed as the solid acid in the fructose dehydration in ionic media, during which the $\mathrm{PTA}(3.0)/\mathrm{MIL}{-101}(\mathrm{Cr})$ showed the best catalytic performance with a $79\%$ yield towards HMF under the optimal reaction conditions. Hatton et al. presented a straightforward protocol to synthesize porous polymer/MOF hybrids for fructose conversion into HMF (Fig. 39).211 The synthesis involves the introduction and polymerization of the monomers (maleimide) and cross-linkers (divinylbenzene) within the MIL-101(Cr) pores to obtain the polymer/MOF composite, followed by bromination. The obtained MOF-PMAi-Br catalyst features large porosity, high surface area and bromine (halamine) groups, which largely accelerated the catalytic activity in the fructose conversion. As a result, an up to $87\%$ HMF yield was obtained under the optimal reaction conditions, which was higher or comparable to those of the reported benchmark catalysts like organometallic salts and metal chlorides.212–215
The saccharides could also be directly upgraded into various value-added chemicals through the facile and efficient cascade or one-pot reactions. For this purpose, adequate multifunctional catalysts including hydrotalcites, silicates, carbons and metal oxides, integrating multiple active sites or functional groups, have been developed.216 In terms of MOF-based materials, Allendorf’s group developed the Ti@IRMOF-74 and Ni@IRMOF-74 catalysts through vapor adsorption of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Cp}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}$ as metal precursors respectively, into the pores of MOF supports, followed by reduction.217 The as-prepared catalysts exhibited good to excellent catalytic performance in the hydrogenolysis of $\scriptstyle\left\{\beta-\mathbf{O}-4\atop\mu\in\mathbf{O}-4\right.$ , ${\scriptstyle\alpha-\mathbf{O}-4}$ and 4-O-5 linkages in aryl-ether compounds involving phenylethyl phenyl ether (PPE), benzyl phenyl ether (BPE), and diphenyl ether (DPE), respectively (Fig. 40). Regardless of the substrate, a maximum conversion of $82\%$ was obtained under the optimal conditions in the absence of base over the Ni@IRMOF-74 catalyst with a $96\%$ selectivity towards hydrogenolysis. Kinetic experiments and DFT calculations suggested that the confinement of Ni active sites within MOF pores significantly contributed to the enhancement of catalytic efficiency and catalyst stability in hydrolysis, thereby extending the practical applications of MOF based catalysts to a new reaction category in biomass valorization.
## 4.3 Transformation of platform molecules
Furfural and HMF are two of the most important biomassderived platform molecules, which could be obtained by cascade acidic hydrolysis of cellulose and hemicellulose present in agricultural and forestry residues and converted into over 80 kinds of value-added chemicals including furfuryl alcohol, levulinic acid, GVL, maleic acid, cyclopentanone, DFF, FDCA etc. Furfuryl alcohol is the most important chemical derived from furfural, the synthesis of which consumes over $60\%$ furfural annual production. Furfuryl alcohol was extensively employed as a reactive solvent, a chemical intermediate, qualified cores as well as molds in the fine chemical industry, polymer industry and foundry industry.
Guan et al. prepared a series of supported $\mathrm{Ru/Zr}$ -MOF catalysts (e.g. UiO-66, UiO-67, Zr6-NDC, MIL-140A, MIL-140B and MIL-140C) and investigated their catalytic efficiency in furfural hydrogenation.218 Among the obtained catalysts, $\mathrm{Ru}/$ UiO-66 outperformed the counterparts and achieved a $94.9\%$ yield of furfuryl alcohol at room temperature, 0.5 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ solvent within $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The high catalytic efficiency was related to the in situ generation of metallic Ru species by hydrogen reduction, which is facilitated by the moderate interactions between Ru and the UiO-66 support. In order to meet the requirements of the furfural hydrogenation-rearrangement reaction and achieve an enhancement of the catalytic efficiency, a bifunctional $\mathbf{Ru}/\mathbf{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ catalyst was prepared by Li et al. through immobilization of Ru NPs with hydrogenation activity on the Lewis acidic MIL-101(Cr) support.219 Kinetic measurements indicated that the Lewis acidic $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites of the MIL-101(Cr) support facilitate the transformation of furfuryl alcohol intermediate into 4-hydroxy-2-cyclopentenone via Piancatelli rearrangement, hereby significantly elevating the catalytic activity and suppressing the generation of tetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol as byproduct. Additionally, the undesirable over hydrogenation of cyclopentanone was also hindered over the catalyst due to the Lewis acid–base interaction. Consequently, a cyclopentanone yield as high as $96\%$ was achieved for the first time over the 3 wt% Ru/MIL-101 catalyst. For further upgrading of cyclopentanone to produce biofuels, Zou et al. reported a highly selective PTA catalyst encapsulated in MOF hosts, which could be facilely prepared through a one-pot hydrothermal strategy.220 The as-prepared $\mathbf{PTA@MIL101(Cr)}$ possessed welldefined octahedral crystallites with smooth external surfaces, this observation being compatible with the encapsulation of PTA molecules within MIL-101(Cr) cages. Benefiting from the confinement within the MOF cages, the PTA molecules are supposed to be uniformly distributed throughout the MOF matrix and isolated as individual molecules in comparison with a PTA/MIL-101(Cr) counterpart prepared following conventional procedures in which PTA is supported on the external surface. No significant PTA agglomeration could be observed for PTA@MIL-101(Cr) even at high loadings (up to $42.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ ). Besides, the confinement effect was also favorable to suppress the undesired di-condensation through the operation of shape selectivity. Thus, over $95\%$ selectivity towards the monocondensed product was achieved over the $42.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ PTA@MIL-101 catalyst in the cyclopentanone transformation.
Aerobic oxidation of furfural with alcohols is one of the promising routes to obtain chemicals and additives for gasoline applications. Tong et al. designed and prepared several MOF-based Pt catalysts like Pt@MOF-5, Pt@UiO-66 and Pt@UiO$66\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ for the oxidation of furfural with ethanol to prepare furan-2-acrolein (Fig. 41).221 The reaction results indicated that
$1\mathrm{wt\%Pt@MOF^{-}{5}(Z n)}$ was the most efficient sample among the as-synthesized catalysts, achieving an $84\%$ furfural conversion and $75.7\%$ furan-2-acrolein yield without any considerable activity loss even after 5 cycles. The high catalytic efficiency of this $1\mathrm{wt\%Pt@MOF^{-}{5}(Z n)}$ catalyst was assigned to the uniform distribution of Pt NPs within the MOF-5(Zn) channels and their synergistic effects. It should be, however, commented that there is abundant literature showing the poor structural stability of MOF- $5(\mathrm{Zn})$ and additional data are necessary to understand the origin of the high catalytic stability.
Hester and co-workers focused on the durability of the catalytic systems and examined in particular the influence of metal encapsulation on the thermal stability of the Pt catalysts.222 In this work, a $\mathbf{PtNP@UiO-67(Zr)}$ catalyst was prepared through the one-pot encapsulation strategy reported by Behrens et al.223 with the addition of benzoic acid as a modulator agent of the structure, morphology and size of UiO-66(Zr). The resultant $\mathbf{PtNP@UiO-67(Zr)}$ catalyst featured small Pt NPs $\left(4{-}6\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ well isolated and uniformly confined within the cavities of UiO-67(Zr) crystals with a monodispersed size of ca. $4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The catalytic activity and stability of the Pt catalyst was examined for the hydrogenation and oxidation of HMF, due to the intrinsic redox abilities of the Pt NPs. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{PtNP@UiO-67(Zr)}$ catalyst showed good activity and selectivity in HMF conversion. However, the thermogravimetric and differential thermal analysis (TG–DTA) indicated that Pt incorporation remarkably impaired the thermal stability of UiO-67(Zr), leading to a ca. $14\%$ drop of the critical temperature in the thermal decomposition test (Fig. 42).
A Pd based catalyst immobilized on the amine-functionalized MOF support, MIL-101(Al)- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , has been prepared via a consecutive anionic exchange and reduction method (Fig. 43).224 During the synthesis, the Pd NPs interact with the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{H}}_{2}$ groups of the MOF ligands, leading to a facile immobilization and uniform distribution of Pd NPs within the MOF pores. The as-prepared $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}}101\mathrm{(AI){-}N H_{2}}$ catalyst showed remarkable catalytic activity for HMF hydrogenation, achieving a complete HMF conversion and $96\%$ DHMTHF yield at room temperature and 1 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ within $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Adsorption measurements suggested that the high selectivity towards DHMTHF derives from the preferential adsorption of the intermediate DHMF with respect to the reactant HMF over the $\mathrm{\mathbf{MIL}}\mathrm{-}101(\mathrm{Al})\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ support. This preferential dihydroxymethylfuran (DHMF) adsorption is caused by the strengthened hydrogen bonding interaction between the hydrophilic DHMF and MOF. However, such high selectivity was observed for five cycles which then dropped dramatically to $80\%$ due to the structural and textural changes of the catalyst.
Chen et al. successfully incorporated the Keggin-type phosphomolybdic acid hydrate $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{PMO}_{12}\mathbf{O}_{40}\right.$ , HPM) within the hierarchical pores of the HKUST-1(Cu) (also known as Cu-BTC) through a one-pot cooperation strategy (Fig. 44).225 The obtained [HKUST][HPM] catalyst combined the advantages of both homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts, achieving a $55\%$ yield of ethoxymethylfurfural (EMF) and $11\%$ yield of ethyl levulinate (EL) in HMF transformation, and could be reused up to five cycles without significant activity loss. Characterization data indicated that such good catalytic efficiency and reusability is due to the uniform dispersion of HPMs and the confinement effect within HKUST hosts.
Recently, Corma et al. designed and fabricated a Hf-based MOF, namely Hf-MOF-808, that was used as a heterogeneous catalyst for the selective aldol condensation of furanic carbonyls with acetone for the preparation of the corresponding methyl ketones.226 The high density of acid–base pairs endowed the catalyst with high efficiency in acetone enolization with weak acid–base pairs via $\mathfrak{a}$ -proton abstraction, affording a nearly quantitative conversion under mild reaction conditions even in the presence of frequent contaminants (e.g. water and acids). Noteworthily, the Hf-MOF-808 catalyst was also active for the transformation of aromatic and aliphatic aldehydes into $\alpha,\beta.$ -unsaturated methyl ketones. Additionally, the Hf-MOF-808 was also evaluated as a robust support for the immobilization of Pd NPs, the resultant Pd@Hf-MOF-808 catalyst were effective in hydrogenation reactions and a one-pot aldol condensation-double bond hydrogenation cascade sequence.
Chen and co-workers reported a supported Ru catalyst on MIL-101(Cr) for levulinic acid hydrogenation to prepare GVL.227 Benefiting from the intrinsic immobilization effect of MIL101(Cr) supports, the small Ru NPs $\left(\sim2.4~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ were uniformly distributed without observing significant aggregation, thereby exhibiting remarkably enhanced hydrogenation reactivity in comparison with Ru/H-ZSM-5 counterparts. As a result, a complete levulinic acid conversion and over $99\%$ GVL selectivity were obtained under ${70~\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}}$ , $1.0\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ within $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ . Similarly, Zhang et al. immobilized Ru NPs onto a sulfonic acid functionalized Zr-based MOF $\mathrm{\UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ to prepare a dual-functional $\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ catalyst.228 The obtained catalyst showed excellent activity and durability in the one-pot hydrogenation– cyclization of methyl levulinate, affording a $100\%$ GVL yield without any significant decrease after five cycles, which outperformed the $\mathrm{Ru/C}$ counterparts. Characterization data suggested that the synergistic effect between the fine Ru NPs $\left(2{\cdot}4\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ and Brønsted acid functionalized $\mathrm{UiO-66(Zr){\cdot}S O_{3}H}$ supports accelerated the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ activation/hydrogenation and the subsequent intramolecular dealcoholation over the catalyst, respectively, therefore leading to the outstanding catalytic performance under relatively mild conditions. Besides, Guan et al. deposited Pd NPs on a pre-synthesized Brønsted acidic functionalized MIL-101-(Cr)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ support with adjustable $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ content for the preparation of a series of $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-}101\mathbf{-}$ (Cr)- $-S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}(x)$ $x=25$ , 50, 80 and 90) materials.229 The obtained $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{MOF}$ composites were subsequently employed as bifunctional catalysts for one-pot hydrodeoxygenation of GVL to yield ethyl valerate. The acidic $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups and Pd NPs endowed the catalyst with considerable activity for ring opening and hydrogenation, leading to a complete conversion of GVL at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 3 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ within $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Additionally, the reactivity data indicated that the high temperature $\left(250~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\right)$ and the high Pd content $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ were essential to achieve the high yield toward ethyl valerate $(83\%)$ , while a lower temperature would bring in the generation of byproducts (e.g. ethyl 4-ethoxypentanoate). However, additional improvements on the catalyst are still needed since the recyclability study revealed a $10\%$ decrease of the product yield after three runs, which was apparently due to the agglomeration of Pd species at the elevated reaction temperatures.
Like furfural and HMF, glycerol is also among the top 14 biomass-derived platform molecules. Glycerol is extensively utilized as a key compound in food production, pharmaceutical industry, polymer manufacture and cosmetic preparation.18–20 Glycerol is available in large quantities and low prices as a byproduct of bio-diesel production and could be upgraded into a large variety of value-added derivatives including dihydroxyacetone, glyoxalic acid and glyceric acid, etc. Zhu et al. designed and fabricated a series of M/MIL-101(Fe)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalysts $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Pd}$ , Pd–Ce, Ce and Au) through a multistep post-synthesis protocol.230
In brief, the pre-synthesized neocuproine was attached onto the $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ groups of $\mathbf{MIL}{\cdot}101\mathbf{\left(Fe\right)}{\cdot}\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ through forming an amide bond. The resultant composites were subsequently used as hosts for the immobilization of different metals to prepare $\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{M}\mathbf{IL-}101(\mathrm{Fe})\mathbf{-NH}_{2}$ catalysts $\mathbf{\hat{M}}=\mathbf{Pd}$ , Pd–Ce, Ce and Au) (Fig. 45). The as-prepared catalysts showed good catalytic activity and durability in glycerol oxidation, achieving a $55\%$ yield toward 1,3-dihydroxyacetone (DHA) over a $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-}C e/M I L\mathrm{-}101(F e)\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ catalyst without significant loss in three recycles.
Vanillin is an important aromatic platform molecule derived from lignin, and could be transformed into a wide range of value-added derivatives including vanillin alcohol and 2-methoxy4-methylphenol through hydrodeoxygenation. Xu and co-workers synthesized a $\mathbf{Pd}\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{MIL-101}(\mathbf{Cr})$ catalyst through the DSM approach for the hydrodeoxygenation of vanillin (Fig. 46).231 Benefiting from the uniform distribution of the ultrafine Pd NPs $\mathrm{1.8\pm0.2\nm}\mathrm{\Omega}$ and steric hindrance of MIL-101(Cr) windows, the obtained catalyst exhibited high catalytic activity and selectivity under mild reaction conditions ( $75^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , 2 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with water as the solvent), attaining a $\sim100\%$ yield of 2-methoxy-4-methylphenol, which was remarkably higher than that of an analogous Pd/MIL-101(Cr) catalyst in which Pd NPs were not within the MIL-101(Cr) pores. Additionally, no obvious activity loss or metal aggregation was observed in the $\mathbf{Pd}(\mathbf{\widehat{a}})\mathbf{MIL}\mathbf{-}101(\mathbf{Cr})$ catalyst after several recycles.
Zhang et al. immobilized Pd NPs on the surface of MIL$101\mathrm{(Cr)}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ via an incipient-wetness impregnation method to synthesize a bifunctional catalyst for vanillin conversion.232 The Brønsted acidity of the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ support led to the uniform distribution of Pd NPs and facilitated the substrate activation process, hence resulting in a nearly quantitative conversion of vanillin into 2-methoxy-4-methylphenol, which was remarkably enhanced as compared with the $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ counterparts (ca. $86\%$ . Soon later, Zhang et al. developed a $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-66}(\mathrm{Zr})–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst through an anionic exchangingreduction approach to incorporate Pd NPs within the aminefunctionalized ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ hosts.233 Due to the influence of the amine groups on the UiO- ${\displaystyle66(\mathrm{Zr})–\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ host, well-defined Pd NPs of $1.5{-}2.5~\mathrm{nm}$ were uniformly formed within the MOF cavities.
$\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-66}(\mathrm{Zr})–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits high durability. The obtained catalyst was highly efficient for vanillin upgrading, affording up to $100\%$ yield towards 2-methoxy-4-methylphenol under optimal conditions at relatively low metal loadings $\left(\mathrm{Pt~}2.5~\mathrm{mol\%}\right)$ ). Afterwards, a 3D hierarchical nanohybrid, namely, PRGO/Ce-MOF, composed of a Ce-based MOF and partially reduced graphene oxide nanosheets (PRGO) was fabricated and utilized as a support to immobilize Pd NPs for vanillin hydrodeoxygenation.234 The covalent bonding and stacking effects between PRGO and Ce-MOF not only enhanced the durability of the obtained Pd/PRGO/Ce-MOF catalyst, but also provided larger specific areas for Pd NP immobilization, therefore leading to enhanced stability and activity in the consecutive hydrodeoxygenation reaction. As a result, the vanillin was exclusively transformed into 2-methoxy-4-methylphenol over the as-prepared Pd/PRGO/ Ce-MOF under mild reaction conditions, and no significant decrease in yield could be observed in four cycles.
Huang et al. demonstrated the fabrication of $\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66(Zr)}.$ $\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ catalysts for cinnamaldehyde hydrogenation (Fig. 47).235 Small Pt nanoclusters were formed into the cavities of UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through an impregnation method. In this procedure, a certain amount of $\mathrm{\bfPt}$ precursor was added to the aqueous suspension of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{UiO-66}}(\mathbf{Zr})\cdot{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ and stirred at room temperature for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . After a reduction treatment under $10\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{Ar}$ flow, a series of $\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66(Zr)–NH_{2}}$ catalysts with metal loadings ranging from 0.97 to $10.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were obtained. Noteworthily, the obtained
Pt clusters were monodispersed within the cavities of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}}).$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and exhibited a small average size of $1.16\pm0.16\mathrm{{nm}}$ even at high Pt loading $\mathrm{(10.7~wt\%)}$ . The Pt-based catalysts were subsequently utilized in the selective hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde, achieving a $98.7\%$ cinnamaldehyde conversion with a $91.7\%$ yield of cinnamyl alcohol over the $10.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathbf{Pt@UiO-66-NH_{2}}$ in the ${10}^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle. The overall accumulative TON was estimated to be as high as 10 900. Such high catalytic efficiency was attributed to the presence of monodispersed ultrafine Pt nanoclusters and the confinement effect of the U $\mathrm{iO-66(Zr){\cdot}N H_{2}}$ host, which enhanced the activity of Pt active sites and hindered side reactions by steric effect.
Li et al. reported a two-step approach for the synthesis of $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ nanocomposites, in which the heterocyclic containing carboxylate ligands of UiO-67(Zr) were used as the stabilizer of guest molecules.236 Typically, the Pd precursors were added into an $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ bpydc containing solution for the preparation of $\mathrm{Pd}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpydc via coordinative bonding to stabilize the $\mathbf{Pd}^{2+}$ ions and prevent their aggregation. Then, the as-prepared hybrid was mixed with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}$ ions and treated under solvothermal conditions for the fabrication of MOFs and in situ encapsulation of Pd species. As expected, this surfactant-free approach facilitated the encapsulation process and omitted complex post treatments. The resultant $\mathbf{Pd}\ @\mathbf{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ catalysts showed high activity in the selective hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde, affording a $100\%$ yield of hydrocinnamaldehyde at room temperature with $0.1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . Such high hydrogenation selectivity to the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds was attributed to the aromatic ring oriented mass diffusion and adsorption of cinnamaldehyde on the catalyst, both of which were suggested to be caused by the $\pi,\pi$ -stacking interaction of aromatic rings. On this basis, Li and co-workers proposed a kinetic modulation strategy for the encapsulation of ‘‘clean’’ metal clusters within MOF cavities.237 This simple one-pot strategy involved direct mixing of $\mathrm{\bf{Pt}}$ and ${\bf U i O-}66({\bf Z r})$ precursors in a DMF solution with acetic acid under a $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ atmosphere. Based on the hard and soft acid and base principle, the in situ generated Pt clusters and oxophilic $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ species tend to coordinate onto the soft C–N and hard $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ groups of DMF, respectively, thereby making the
DMF molecules a bridge between Pt and MOF to accelerate the anisotropic growth of MOF, meanwhile preventing the selfnucleation (Fig. 48). As a result, the obtained $\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66(Zr)}$ nanocomposites exhibited a typical core–shell structure with the Pt clusters fully embedded within the UiO-66(Zr) shells. Accordingly, the MOF shells played a vital molecular sieving role in the catalytic oxidation of cinnamyl alcohol, affording $100\%$ selectivity to cinnamyl aldehyde at quantitative conversions.
In another work, Li et al. immobilized Pt NPs on the Lewis acidic MOF MIL-101(Cr) for the preparation of a Pt/MIL-101(Cr) catalyst that was used for the selective hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde.238 Owing to the strong interaction between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ groups and Pt NPs as well as the high hydrogen isolation capacity of the latter, the obtained catalyst showed high efficiency in the selective hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde into hydrocinnamaldehyde, achieving an over $99.9\%$ yield at room temperature and atmospheric $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure. A prolonged reaction time only resulted in the observation of trace amounts of byproducts, as the over-hydrogenation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ groups was highly disfavored by their strong adsorption on the Lewis acidic Cr sites of MIL-101, a proposal that was supported by in situ ATR-IR spectroscopy.
## 5. MOF-derived catalysts
Besides the previously described strategies based on the use of pristine or modified MOFs as catalysts or the fabrication of composite materials in which one of the components is a MOF, a new type of catalyst consisting of metal or metal oxide NPs wrapped on doped carbons has been also developed using MOFs as precursors. The process generally consists in the thermolysis of the MOF under an inert atmosphere to transform the organic linkers into some carbon, while the metal ions undergo simultaneously a chemical reduction/oxidation to its metallic/oxidic state. It is commonly observed that the morphology of the MOF precursor is maintained during the transformation. This methodology has been adapted to the preparation of bimetallic alloys with controlled atomic ratios and to different nanostructures with a yolk–shell or multishell configuration. Since metal NPs are general catalysts for hydrogenations, reductions and oxidations, the MOF-derived catalysts also find application for these reactions in the field of biomass transformation. Table 6 collects those reports in which MOF-derived carbon-wrapped metal NPs have been used as catalysts for biomass conversion.
## 5.1 Transformation of lignocellulosic biomass
Tusˇar et al. proposed a MOF-derived in situ generated Ni catalyst for the hydrodeoxygenation of liquified lignocellulosic biomass.239 The MIL-77(Ni) prepared through the solvothermal method was employed as the precursor and directly mixed with the liquified lignocellulosic biomass and tetralin liquid phase. The mixture was sealed, purged with 8 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and heated to
$300~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ under vigorous stirring. During the reaction, the unstable MIL-77(Ni) template decomposed to form the Ni NPs, which showed high efficiency in lignocellulosic biomass transformation to yield polar and non-polar products. Noteworthily, this MOF-derived Ni catalyst featured a ten-fold enhanced mass activity in comparison with the commercial $\mathbf{Ni}/\mathbf{SiO}_{2}–\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst. This higher activity of the in situ formed Ni NPs wrapped by carbon could be attributed to the well-determined MOF structure with high purity and crystallinity.
## 5.2 Transformation of C5/C6 sugars
Li and co-workers demonstrated a facile and environmentally benign two-step approach involving heat treatment and a sulfonation step for the synthesis of a MOF-5 derived solid acid carbonaceous catalyst, namely, MDC- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ , which exhibits catalytic activity for fructose dehydration (Fig. 49).240 The MOF-5 precursor was first prepared through the solvothermal method and then pyrolyzed under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere at elevated temperatures from 200 to $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to evaporate the Zn species and increase the pore size. Subsequently, the resultant MOF-derived carbons were subjected to a sulfonation process for the introduction of $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. The as-prepared catalyst possessed hierarchical pores and Brønsted acidic $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, hence exhibiting an enhanced catalytic performance in fructose dehydration, affording an up to $89.57\%$ yield of HMF at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a isopropanol–DMSO solvent.
## 5.3 Transformation of platform molecules
Metal–organic polyhedra (MOPs) are a new class of coordination complexes fabricated by metal nodes and organic ligands, analogous to the metal nodes forming MOFs. As in the case of MOFs, the inherent well-defined and symmetric nanostructure of MOPs endowed them with ample potential as templates for the preparation of discrete ultrafine metal NPs. Li et al. proposed a confined thermolysis approach to in situ convert the pre-encapsulated MOP precursors into the N-decorated carbon– Pd hybrids ranging from single atom to sub-nanoclusters (Fig. 50).241 For demonstration of the concept, the waterstable MIL-101 and Pd-based $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ (M stands for $\mathrm{(en)Pd(NO_{3})_{2}}$ ; L stands for 1,3,5-tris(4-pyridyl)-2,4,6-triazine) were selected as the MOF host and MOP template, respectively. In brief, certain amounts of the M and L precursors were successively introduced into the mesopores of MIL-101, undergoing selfassembly to form the $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ template. Subsequently, a thermal treatment under hydrogen flow was carried out to obtain the $\mathbf{Pd}/\mathbf{C}/-\mathbf{N}\subset\mathbf{MIL}/-101$ $\left(\mathbf{PCN}{\mathbf{\Lambda}}{\mathbf{\Lambda}}\mathbf{{\Lambda}}\mathbf{M}\right)$ constituted by C-N-decorated Pd single atoms and sub-nanoclusters within the MIL-101 host. It is worth mentioning that the resultant catalyst with $0.64~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pd (denoted as $0.64\mathrm{PCN}{\bf\zeta}=\bf{M},$ exhibited superior catalytic performance in the hydrogenation of furfural, affording a ${>}99\%$ cyclopentanone yield under relatively mild conditions ( $\mathrm{~\'~}_{1}\mathrm{atm}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ).
Xiao’s group reported a MOF-derived $\mathbf{CuNi_{0.5}@C}$ catalyst that was also employed to promote hydrogenation of furfural into cyclopentanone. The $\mathbf{CuNi_{0.5}@C}$ material was prepared through the pyrolysis of the Ni impregnated Cu-BTC crystals under a nitrogen atmosphere.242 The as-prepared catalyst possessed highly dispersed metallic Cu and Ni NPs embedded in a porous carbon matrix with a well-retained octahedral morphology, which largely contributed to its catalytic performance. Upon addition of Ni metal, the $\mathbf{CuNi_{0.5}@C}$ catalyst exhibited a dramatically enhanced catalytic efficiency in comparison with the Cu-BTC derived Cu-based counterpart, affording a $99.3\%$ furfural conversion and a $96.9\%$ cyclopentanone yield at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 5 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in the aqueous phase within $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ . Through a similar approach, the same group also reported a bimetallic $\operatorname{CuCo/C}$ catalyst for furfural hydrogenation, obtaining ca. $96.4\%$ yield of furfuryl alcohol. However, the activity of the $\operatorname{CuCo/C}$ catalyst gradually decreased within four cycles as a result of coke formation.243 A Ni/C catalyst prepared through the direct pyrolysis of Ni-BTC under a nitrogen atmosphere has been reported by Wang et al. for the hydrogenation of furfural to furfuryl alcohol and tetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol (Fig. 51).244 During the pyrolysis, the Ni species were converted into Ni NPs via Ni carbides as intermediates, which were determined as the hydrogenation active sites and stabilizing agents, respectively. Characterization data indicated that the Ni NPs sizes $\left(13-37~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ 1 followed an inverse correlation with the pyrolysis temperature $(400-700\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ and Ni carbide content. Noteworthily, the Ni/C-500 catalyst, featuring the optimal Ni carbides content, outperformed other as-prepared catalysts in furfural hydrogenation, affording nearly quantitative yield of tetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 MPa $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Tong et al. demonstrated the preparation of two Co-based catalysts (ACS-I and ACS-II) via the removal of coordinated water and pyrolysis of the same 2D Co-MOF template, respectively.245 Both catalysts exhibited outstanding catalytic performance in the oxidative condensation of furfural, affording a maximum $84.9\%$ furfural conversion and $99\%$ selectivity to 3-(furan-2-yl-)-2-methylacryl aldehyde, respectively. Such attractive catalytic performance was attributed to the abundant nanocomposites as active sites in ACS-I and ACS-II, respectively. Wang’s group introduced a bimetallic NiCo-MOF as the sacrificial template into $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ supports and prepared a $\mathrm{NiCo}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ -MOF catalyst through pyrolysis.246 The obtained NiCo NPs of subnanometer size were uniformly distributed within $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ cavities and exhibited a relatively high catalytic efficiency in the subsequent hydrogenation of furfuryl alcohol to tetrahydrofurfuryl alcohol (THFA), affording a $99.8\%$ furfuryl alcohol conversion and $99.1\%$ selectivity towards THFA. These activity data outperform those of analogous counterparts prepared through impregnation methods. The outstanding catalytic performance of the as-prepared NiCo catalysts was attributed to the enhanced exposure of active NiCo sites with strong synergistic effects on the catalyst surface. A facile strategy for the fabrication of yolk– shell $\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}$ was reported by Li’s group and the material was used as a catalyst for aerobic oxidation of alcohols.247 In brief, ZIF-67 cores were covered with ZIF-8 shells for the formation of core–shell ZIF-67@ZIF-8 hybrids, which were employed as templates. Pyrolysis of these $\mathrm{ZIF{-}}67{\textcircled{\div}}\mathrm{ZIF{-}}8$ precursors yield yolk–shell $\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}$ nanostructures with Co NPs encapsulated in ZIF-8 derived N-decorated carbon layers. The resultant $\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{\mathbf{a}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}$ nanostructures were employed as nanoreactors in the aerobic oxidation of an extensive range of alcohols including biomassderived furfuryl alcohol and HMF, exhibiting good to excellent catalytic activities. Such attractive catalytic performance was attributed to the strong synergisms between Co NPs and carbon nanosheets. Additionally, the ZIF-8 derived carbon layers also contribute to the durability of the $\mathrm{Co}\textcircled{\mathrm{{a}}\mathrm{{C}-\mathrm{{N}}}}$ catalyst through isolation and confinement of Co NPs. Furthermore, the multishelled nanostructures, possessing higher surface area, multiphase heterogeneous interfaces and improved mass diffusion should also have large application in biomass transformation reactions.248,249 Recently, a general strategy using multi-shelled MOF hybrids as sacrificial templates was proposed by Li and co-workers for the fabrication of multi-shelled hollow, yolkmulti-shelled hollow nanostructures with controllable morphologies and tunable components (Fig. 52).250 Taking the fabrication of multi-shelled hollow $\mathrm{Co}\ @\mathrm{NC}$ dodecahedra as an example, the synthesis involves the step by step epitaxial growth of the ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 layers and subsequent pyrolysis to prepare a series of multi-layered solid ZIF(8) hybrids, namely, 2L-, 4L-, 6L-, and 8L-ZIFs(8), as templates and the corresponding multi-shelled hollow Co NPs within N-doped carbons, denoted as 1LH-, 2LH-, 3LH-, and 4LH-Co@NC, respectively. Besides, this two-step approach was also effective for the fabrication of yolkmulti-shelled hollow $\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{NC}$ and multi-shelled hollow metalalloy $\textcircled{\mathrm{2}\mathrm{NC}}$ nanostructures by substituting the multilayer solid ZIF(8) by multilayer solid ZIF(67) and metal doping, respectively. The catalytic activity of the resultant multi-shelled nanostructures was tested for the selective hydrogenation of furfural into cyclopentanol, achieving a yield as high as $97\%$ for cyclopentanol using the 4LH-Co@NC catalyst under optimal reaction conditions. Such remarkable catalytic performance of the 4LH$\mathbf{Co}\ @\mathbf{NC}$ catalyst was attributed to the favorable mass diffusion, the positive influence of multiphase heterogeneous interfaces and the existence of abundant metal active sites with high dispersion in comparison with the single shell or solid counterparts.
Li and co-workers proposed a two-step approach for the preparation of a series of MOF-derived ultrafine metal oxides NPs confined in the porous silicate supports (Fig. 53).251 Different MOF precursors containing Co, Fe, Ni and Cu were initially introduced into the 3D channels of KIT-6 to prepare MOF@KIT-6 templates, which were subsequently calcined at low temperatures under oxygen flow to remove the organic components and promote the in situ conversion of the metal ions into the corresponding metal oxides NPs. The obtained $\mathbf{M@KIT-6}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{M}_{\lambda}}=\mathbf{Co}$ , Fe, Ni and Cu) nanostructures were constituted by highly dispersed ultrafine metal oxides NPs $\left(2\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ confined within the mesopores of KIT-6, without observing significant aggregation even at high metal loadings (up to $16.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ ). Hence, the obtained catalysts exhibited high catalytic efficiency in HMF aerobic oxidation to produce FDCA. Noteworthily, under optimal conditions, a nearly quantitative FDCA yield was obtained over the Co@KIT-6 catalyst without any activity loss in six cycles.
The same group also designed and prepared an acidic $\mathbf{Fe/C}{-}\mathbf{S}$ catalyst through pyrolysis of an MIL-88B template at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a CO atmosphere (Fig. 54). The resultant catalyst exhibited monodispersed particles of octahedral morphology and preferentially exposed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\bf O}_{4}(111)$ crystal facets covered with sulfur doped carbon layers.252 The $\mathbf{Fe/C}{-}\mathbf{S}$ catalyst showed outstanding catalytic efficiency in the one-pot transformation of fructose into DFF with HMF as the intermediate. A complete fructose conversion and over $99\%$ yield of DFF were achieved under optimal conditions. The formation of DFF in high yields was attributed to the presence of non-oxidized sulfur atoms and the low adsorption energy of DFF on the catalyst, which facilitated fructose hydrolysis and the selective oxidation of the intermediate HMF. Additionally, reusability tests did not show any significant activity loss after six recycles.
In another work, Li et al. employed a Fe–Co based MOF (MIL-45b) as the precursor to prepare a bimetallic Fe–Co catalyst via pyrolysis. The resulting material was used as a catalyst for the selective oxidation of HMF.250 According to the Kirkendall effects, MIL-45b decomposed at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to form a unique hollow structure composed of FeCo oxides and a trace amount of metallic Fe and Co species. The unique hollow structures promoted the preferential adsorption of HMF and the fast desorption of DFF, hence resulting in a ${>}99\%$ yield towards DFF, which remained up to five recycles without any significant decrease. Wu et al. reported for the first time the metal-free catalysis of HMF to FDCA conversion using an N-doped nanoporous carbon (NNC), which was derived from ZIF-8 through the pyrolysis and acid etching process.254 The as-prepared NNC catalyst featured enhanced content of graphitic nitrogen (N-Q) in comparison with the conventional N-doped carbon materials, and thereby exhibited significantly promoted catalytic performance in the aerobic oxidation of HMF, achieving an up to $80\%$ FDCA yield at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient oxygen pressure. Hu et al. reported a 2D $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{3}$ nanoflake-like material for HMF oxidation to yield FDCA.255 The Mn-based catalyst, namely, M400, was facilely prepared through the pyrolysis of Mn-PTA (a Mn-based MOF) and featured a unique holey flake nanostructure, which was assumed to be related to the loss of MOF ligands and oxidation of Mn cations during the heat treatment. As expected, the M400 exhibited remarkable catalytic performance, affording $>99.5\%$ FDCA yield at complete conversion of HMF, which was superior to those of the commercial $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ catalysts. Wang and co-workers prepared a MOFderived sulfonated porous carbon for the conversion of HMF into EMF and EL.256 In brief, Cu-BTC was employed as the sacrificial template and upon carbonization, the resultant composite was further treated with sulfuric acid for the removal of Cu species and sulfonation to yield the $\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ catalyst. Benefiting from the unique porous nanostructure and abundant Brønsted $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, the catalyst showed remarkable catalytic performance in HMF conversion. Interestingly, the major product could be simply modified through tailoring the reaction conditions, and an up to $71\%$ and $81\%$ yield of EMF and EL was obtained under optimal conditions, respectively.
A MOF-derived $\gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst was reported by Shen et al. and used for the selective dehydration of glycerol to acrolein.257 In the synthesis, an Al-based MOF (MIL-96) was initially prepared through the solvothermal synthesis and subsequently calcined at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to decompose, giving rise to $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ . The as-prepared $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ catalyst exhibited remarkable catalytic performance in the dehydration of glycerol, achieving a higher selectivity $(74\%)$ towards acrolein in comparison with the conventionally prepared $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ nanorods $(72\%)$ or particles $(55\%)$ . The higher activity of $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ was attributed to the presence of mesopores throughout the nanocrystal and the accessibility to the abundant acid sites.
Jiang et al. immobilized Pd NPs within ZIF-8-derived N-doped porous carbons (NPC) for the synthesis of Pd/NPC-ZIF-8, which was subsequently employed in the hydrodeoxygenation of vanillin in the aqueous phase (Fig. 55).258 The ZIF-8-derived NPC supports endowed the Pd catalyst with high graphitic-N content, high surface area, hierarchical porosity and high hydrophilicity, which largely promoted the electron transfer to Pd sites, favored immobilization of fine Pd NPs (ca. $1\mathrm{nm}$ ), facilitated mass diffusion, and strengthened the interaction between the reactant and catalyst, thus remarkably boosting its catalytic performance. As expected, a quantitative amount of 2-methoxy-4-methylphenol was obtained from vanillin over the Pd/NPC-ZIF-8 catalyst with a turnover frequency (TOF) value as high as $100\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under optimal conditions, without obvious activity loss within three cycles.
ILs are a class of green and functional solvents, whose physicochemical properties could be facilely tailored through the appropriate combination or modification of the cations and anions. ILs and their derivatives have been widely employed as catalysts in biomass related transformations and have exhibited attractive performances. Recently, Li and co-workers reported a facile strategy for the preparation of encapsulated N-decorated Au catalysts through a confined pyrolysis approach using an Au-based IL as the metal resource and template (Fig. 56).259 In brief, a methanol solution of the pre-synthesized $[\mathrm{Bmim}][\mathrm{AuCl_{4}}]$ (Bmim: 1-butyl-3-methylimidazole) was mixed with the activated ZIF-8 under stirring for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature; the mixture was stirred for another $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under high vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ to confine the IL within ZIF-8 pores. After filtration and methanol washing, the resultant sacrificial template was calcined at a certain temperature under argon flow for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ to yield the $\mathrm{\small{\left[Bmim\right]}}[\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}]\ @Z I F–8–x–T$ catalysts, where $x$ and $T$ indicated the weight ratio of IL to ZIF-8 and calcination temperature, respectively. During the pyrolysis, the confinement effect of ZIF-8 pores effectively hindered the metal migration and aggregation, and the presence and decoration of N species was also beneficial for the improvement of Au NP dispersion. Consequently, the resultant Au catalysts were highly dispersed N-decorated Au NPs ranging from ca. 2.91 to $3.69\ \mathrm{nm}$ confined in the cavities of the ZIF-8 derived carbon matrix. Noteworthily, as compared with Au catalysts prepared followed conventional procedures, the obtained $[\mathrm{Bmim][AuCl_{4}]@Z I F-8-6.25\%-900}$ exhibited significantly enhanced catalytic efficiency in aerobic oxidation of a wide scope of alcohols into aldehydes. Specifically biomass-derived cinnamyl alcohol renders ca. $25\%$ yield of cinnamaldehyde under the optimized reaction conditions.
## 6. Conclusions and prospects
Considering the increasing concerns on sustainability, the use of biomass derived fuels, chemicals and materials will steadily grow in the coming years, representing a larger share of the total chemical production. To be viable, biomass conversion requires selective, robust and affordable catalysts, preferably based on abundant elements. Following the lead of petrochemistry, heterogeneous catalysis is largely based on porous materials, zeolites being an example of efficient and sustainable catalysts for vapor phase, high temperature conversion of hydrocarbons. However, due to the limited pore size of conventional zeolites, these crystalline porous materials exhibit much less activity for liquid phase reactions involving larger size multifunctional substrates, as those constituting biomass.
In this context, the present review has shown the high flexibility in the design and adaptation that MOFs offer as alternative porous solid catalysts to promote biomass transformations, including hydrolysis and depolymerization of cellulose and lignin models, as well as isomerization, dehydrations, hydrogenations and oxidations. Examples from the literature have been described showing the superior catalytic activity of MOFs compared to zeolites and other solid catalysts.
The current state of the art of biomass conversion is dominated by acid catalysis as well as hydrogenation and oxidation. In the review, emphasis has been made to show the various possibilities that MOFs offer to tune the acidity, structure and activity to develop solid catalysts that are frequently compared with or perform better than the benchmark catalysts for each reaction. In this review, the existing literature has been organized from the use of pristine MOFs towards more complex catalytic systems, including multicomponent composites and MOF-derived metal and metal oxides wrapped on doped carbons. The purpose was to convey to the reader the wide range of tools that can be applied in MOF catalysis. These tools go from a judicious selection of metals, introduction of substituents at the linker, guest incorporation, post-synthetic modification, preparation of hybrid systems and pyrolysis or calcination, among some possibilities. Novel advances in the field of MOF catalysis, particularly the use of 2D MOFs that appear to be more stable and active than analogous 3D materials, or MOP that can be considered perhaps as 0D MOF, could bring new opportunities for further increasing the catalytic activity. Also, MOFs offer opportunities in biomass beyond conventional catalysis as electrocatalysts and photocatalysts.
Biomass polymers and monomers are multifunctional organic molecules. Frequently the wanted platform compound or chemical differs in several functional groups. Conventional approaches are based on consecutive functional group transformation. However, from the point of view of process intensification, tandem and cascade reactions in which more than one elementary transformation takes place in the same process using a multifunctional catalyst represents a considerable advantage, simplifying workups and diminishing the number of steps. This review has commented on some examples in which MOFs have been used as multifunctional catalysts to promote tandem reactions. In several of these examples, acidity is combined with hydrogenation or oxidation reactions and, therefore, acid MOFs containing transition metal NPs are the most used catalysts for these tandem reactions. Taking into account the commented versatility and flexibility offered by MOFs to encompass more than one type of active site, it can be predicted that tandem reactions would also be an area that will expand considerably in the coming years with additional examples. Performing more than two processes concomitantly with the same MOF material should also be possible.
In addition, continuous flow and upscaling are frequently required steps in the way to the development of commercial industrial processes. In this regard, it is clear that biomass conversion offers considerable opportunities to develop novel industrial processes, since they are more recent reactions than well-established petrochemical transformations. It would be a milestone in MOF application, and heterogeneous catalysis in general, if an industrial biomass transformation process using a MOF as a catalyst could be developed, because it will constitute a clear case of MOF exhibiting a superior performance than any other alternative catalyst. In view of the existing literature, it appears that this possibility of having a biomass industrial process using a MOF as a catalyst should be seriously considered, provided that the necessary steps give also positive results.
